{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HLHGMqLfcZ4z1GHZmcNg93","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — meta_brain_lora_routing\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: CoRL 2026 (Austin, Nov 9-12)\n5\t- Abstract DDL: 2026-05-26\n6\t- Paper DDL: 2026-05-29\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\t\n10\tVLA internal representations already encode rich perturbation-type-specific information (Phase 0: single-layer F1=1.0 at K=1), yet existing robustness methods either ignore this diagnostic signal entirely (VLA-GSE, RobustVLA) or only use it for detection without correction (SAFE). Meta-Brain closes the loop: diagnose perturbation type from frozen activations, then route to a type-specialized LoRA expert for targeted correction — achieving the \"right fix for the right failure\" at ~10x fewer parameters than perturbation-agnostic capacity routing.\n11\t\n12\t## Core Claims (3)\n13\t\n14\t1. **Perturbation Diagnosability** — Frozen VLA (pi0) internal activations at a single layer and single token (PaliGemma layer 6, first token) encode perturbation type with perfect accuracy (3-class K=1 F1=1.000 with first-token pooling), enabling real-time, zero-overhead perturbation diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n15\t\n16\t2. **Targeted Correction > Uniform Correction** — Perturbation-type-specific LoRA experts (Visual/Spatial/Physical), routed by the diagnosed type, outperform a universal LoRA baseline of equivalent total parameter budget on LIBERO-Plus, demonstrating that routing by perturbation type is a more efficient use of adaptation capacity than uniform correction.\n17\t\n18\t3. **Parameter-Efficient Robustness** — Meta-Brain (~12M trainable params) achieves robustness gains that a parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank 48, ~12M) cannot replicate, demonstrating that routing by perturbation type is more efficient use of adaptation capacity than uniform correction. Cross-backbone comparison with VLA-GSE (114M on Qwen3-VL-4B) is indirect/suggestive only — primary efficiency metric is within-backbone gain-over-parameter-matched-baseline.\n19\t\n20\t## Story Spine\n21\t\n22\t- **Problem**: VLAs achieve >90% on standard LIBERO but collapse under perturbation (pi0: 53.6% on LIBERO-Plus). Different perturbation types disrupt different processing stages — visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations invalidate action trajectories, physical perturbations break force assumptions. Yet all existing robustness methods apply either single-modality correction or perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation.\n23\t\n24\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: VLA-GSE (114M params, 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus) demonstrates routing helps, but routes by per-token capacity needs — the same gating configuration handles camera (64.4%) and layout (82.6%) without perturbation awareness. FTM achieves 90% on visual perturbations with 4K params but cannot handle spatial/physical. RobustVLA adds +12.6% via uniform adversarial training. No method combines perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction.\n25\t\n26\t- **Our framing**: We propose perturbation-diagnostic routing as a new design dimension for VLA robustness, orthogonal to capacity-based routing (VLA-GSE), rank-based adaptation (LoRA-SP), and task-based routing (MoIRA/CORAL). We show that VLA internals already \"know\" what went wrong — they just lack the mechanism to apply the right fix. Meta-Brain adds that mechanism with minimal parameters.\n27\t\n28\t- **Headline result template**: \"On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves X% with ~12M trainable parameters, improving Y% over frozen pi0 baseline and Z% over a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (~12M), demonstrating that perturbation-diagnostic routing outperforms uniform adaptation at equivalent parameter budget.\"\n29\t\n30\t## Contribution Structure (single system contribution, not 3 independent claims)\n31\t\n32\tOne well-executed systems contribution: Meta-Brain — a modular framework that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA activations and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts. Supporting evidence:\n33\t1. **Empirical finding**: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity (prerequisite observation)\n34\t2. **Architecture + ablation**: PAR router + LoRA bank with comprehensive ablation ladder including parameter-matched controls (rank-48 universal), random routing baseline, and rank scaling analysis\n35\t3. **Practical insight**: Layer placement analysis reveals perturbation-type-to-layer mapping (V→early, S→mid, P→late)\n36\t\n37\tNote: Correction Memory is an optional module — present as ablation footnote, NOT as a named architectural component. Dream reviewers across all tiers viewed CM (+1-2%) as padding.\n38\t\n39\t## Venue Fit (CoRL 2026)\n40\t\n41\t**Strengths**:\n42\t- Pi0 backbone + LIBERO-Plus eval is core CoRL territory\n43\t- Architecture operates on representations (sim evaluation methodologically sufficient)\n44\t- Parameter efficiency matters for real-robot deployment (~12M vs 114M)\n45\t- Single forward pass routing = no latency overhead for real-time control\n46\t\n47\t**Risks**:\n48\t- Sim-only evaluation (mitigated: LIBERO-Plus is CVPR 2026 standard, sim-only is accepted pattern at CoRL for architecture contributions)\n49\t- Single backbone (pi0) — need gain-over-baseline framing to enable contextualized comparison with VLA-GSE (Qwen3-VL-4B)\n50\t- Absolute performance may trail SRPO (82.1%) / CorridorVLA (83.21%) which use full training — frame as parameter-efficient approach, not absolute SOTA chase\n51\t\n52\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n53\t\n54\t### C1: \"Phase 0 classification is too easy — trivially solvable routing\"\n55\t**Risk**: High Phase 0 accuracy (F1=0.993) may suggest the task is trivial and routing adds no value.\n56\t**Response**: Easy diagnosis ≠ easy correction. The value of routing is in Phase 1 (specialized vs universal LoRA), not Phase 0. Easy routing is actually desirable — it means the router adds zero overhead concern. Ablation exp-005 directly tests whether routing matters.\n57\t\n58\t### C2: \"Sim-only evaluation, no real robot\"\n59\t**Risk**: CoRL values real-world experiments.\n60\t**Response**: LIBERO-Plus is the standard robustness benchmark (CVPR 2026). Meta-Brain operates on representations, not hardware-specific signals. Sim-only is the established evaluation pattern for VLA architecture contributions at CoRL (SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA all sim-only on LIBERO variants).\n61\t\n62\t### C3: \"Single backbone (pi0), limited generalizability\"\n63\t**Risk**: Results may be specific to pi0's PaliGemma architecture.\n64\t**Response**: (a) Phase 0 finding (perturbation classifiability) is grounded in mechanistic evidence across multiple VLAs (SAFE on OpenVLA, probing on OpenVLA, feature steering on pi0.5 + OpenVLA). (b) Tier 3 cross-backbone experiment on SmolVLA/OpenVLA if time permits. (c) Gain-over-baseline metric enables fair comparison across backbones.\n65\t\n66\t### C4: \"Parameter budget comparison unfair — different backbone from VLA-GSE\"\n67\t**Risk**: Comparing 6M (on pi0) vs 114M (on Qwen3-VL-4B) is apples-to-oranges.\n68\t**Response**: Compare gain-over-respective-LoRA-baseline: VLA-GSE gains +12.0% over LoRA (69.2% → 81.2%); Meta-Brain should gain X% over universal LoRA on pi0. Parameter efficiency = gain per trainable parameter. Also report absolute numbers with caveat.\n69\t\n70\t### C5: \"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\"\n71\t**Risk**: Lumping camera+light+background+noise into \"Visual\" may lose useful distinctions.\n72\t**Response**: Phase 0 6-class analysis shows intra-Visual perturbations are more similar to each other than to Spatial/Physical. 3-class matches the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing (early/mid/late layers). Ablation: 6-class routing vs 3-class routing comparison.\n73\t\n74\t### C6: \"Correction Memory adds complexity for marginal gain\"\n75\t**Risk**: CM may seem over-engineered for routing-weight modulation.\n76\t**Response**: CM is presented as an optional module. Ablation (with/without CM) quantifies its contribution. CM adds only 1 learned parameter (alpha) + CPU-bound FAISS.\n77\t\n78\t### C7: \"Unseen perturbation types at deployment — open-set robustness\"\n79\t**Risk**: 3-class taxonomy is fixed. Novel perturbation types (weather, sensor degradation) will be force-classified to nearest known type, potentially routing to wrong expert.\n80\t**Response**: (a) PAR outputs a probability distribution — low-confidence predictions (high entropy) can trigger fallback to universal LoRA or no correction, providing a natural open-set rejection mechanism. (b) The 3-class taxonomy covers the major perturbation axes in robotic manipulation; truly novel perturbations are an explicit limitation we acknowledge. (c) Correction Memory provides partial adaptation — retrieved exemplars from similar but not identical perturbations can modulate routing appropriately.\n81\t\n82\t### C8: \"Training-deployment feature dimension mismatch\"\n83\t**Risk**: Phase 0 classifier trained on episode-averaged activations (~44 steps) but router uses single-step (K=1) features.\n84\t**Response**: K=1 few-shot analysis directly validates this: F1=0.898 overall, layer 6 K=1=1.0. Paper must explicitly state: (a) classifier is trained on episode-averaged features for label efficiency, (b) deployed router uses single-step features, (c) K=1 validation bridges this gap. Phase 1 PAR router will be trained directly on single-step features to eliminate any residual gap.\n85\t\n86\t## Baselines & Comparisons\n87\t\n88\t| Method | Type | Params | Expected Comparison |\n89\t|--------|------|--------|-------------------|\n90\t| Frozen pi0 | Lower bound | 0 | Our improvement over this = headline number |\n91\t| Universal LoRA (rank 16) | Ablation | ~3.7M | Same per-expert capacity, no routing — tests Claim 2 |\n92\t| Meta-Brain (PAR + ALB) | Ours | ~12M | Full system (3x3.7M experts + 0.5M router) |\n93\t| Meta-Brain + CM | Ours (full) | ~12M + 1 | With Correction Memory |\n94\t| Random routing | Ensemble control | ~12M | Random expert assignment — tests if routing matters vs ensemble effect |\n95\t| Oracle routing | Upper bound | ~12M | Perfect routing — gap to Meta-Brain = routing quality |\n96\t| FTM/FLA | Visual-only | 4K-4.7M | Strong on visual, cannot do spatial/physical |\n97\t| VLA-GSE | Capacity routing | 114M | Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), compare gain-over-LoRA |\n98\t| RobustVLA | Adversarial training | Full model | Uniform approach, compare robustness gain |\n99\t\n100\t## Key Figures (Planned)\n101\t\n102\t1. **Architecture diagram**: Frozen pi0 → PAR (layer 6) → soft routing → LoRA-V/S/P bank → corrected output\n103\t2. **Phase 0 evidence**: t-SNE of layer 6 activations colored by perturbation type + confusion matrix\n104\t3. **Main result table**: LIBERO-Plus per-perturbation breakdown (7 categories × methods)\n105\t4. **Ablation ladder**: Bar chart — Frozen → Universal LoRA → Random Routing → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n106\t5. **Parameter efficiency**: Scatter plot — gain-over-baseline vs trainable parameters (Meta-Brain vs VLA-GSE vs FTM vs RobustVLA)\n107\t6. **Routing analysis**: Heatmap of PAR soft routing weights per perturbation type (should be near-diagonal)\n108\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90281699-6fb6-4e6d-b094-53549aa733d5","uuid":"65f06fef-a555-4b10-9f30-0af3fe938135","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:01:00.508Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T165902/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — meta_brain_lora_routing\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: CoRL 2026 (Austin, Nov 9-12)\n- Abstract DDL: 2026-05-26\n- Paper DDL: 2026-05-29\n\n## Core Insight\n\nVLA internal representations already encode rich perturbation-type-specific information (Phase 0: single-layer F1=1.0 at K=1), yet existing robustness methods either ignore this diagnostic signal entirely (VLA-GSE, RobustVLA) or only use it for detection without correction (SAFE). Meta-Brain closes the loop: diagnose perturbation type from frozen activations, then route to a type-specialized LoRA expert for targeted correction — achieving the \"right fix for the right failure\" at ~10x fewer parameters than perturbation-agnostic capacity routing.\n\n## Core Claims (3)\n\n1. **Perturbation Diagnosability** — Frozen VLA (pi0) internal activations at a single layer and single token (PaliGemma layer 6, first token) encode perturbation type with perfect accuracy (3-class K=1 F1=1.000 with first-token pooling), enabling real-time, zero-overhead perturbation diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n\n2. **Targeted Correction > Uniform Correction** — Perturbation-type-specific LoRA experts (Visual/Spatial/Physical), routed by the diagnosed type, outperform a universal LoRA baseline of equivalent total parameter budget on LIBERO-Plus, demonstrating that routing by perturbation type is a more efficient use of adaptation capacity than uniform correction.\n\n3. **Parameter-Efficient Robustness** — Meta-Brain (~12M trainable params) achieves robustness gains that a parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank 48, ~12M) cannot replicate, demonstrating that routing by perturbation type is more efficient use of adaptation capacity than uniform correction. Cross-backbone comparison with VLA-GSE (114M on Qwen3-VL-4B) is indirect/suggestive only — primary efficiency metric is within-backbone gain-over-parameter-matched-baseline.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: VLAs achieve >90% on standard LIBERO but collapse under perturbation (pi0: 53.6% on LIBERO-Plus). Different perturbation types disrupt different processing stages — visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations invalidate action trajectories, physical perturbations break force assumptions. Yet all existing robustness methods apply either single-modality correction or perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: VLA-GSE (114M params, 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus) demonstrates routing helps, but routes by per-token capacity needs — the same gating configuration handles camera (64.4%) and layout (82.6%) without perturbation awareness. FTM achieves 90% on visual perturbations with 4K params but cannot handle spatial/physical. RobustVLA adds +12.6% via uniform adversarial training. No method combines perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction.\n\n- **Our framing**: We propose perturbation-diagnostic routing as a new design dimension for VLA robustness, orthogonal to capacity-based routing (VLA-GSE), rank-based adaptation (LoRA-SP), and task-based routing (MoIRA/CORAL). We show that VLA internals already \"know\" what went wrong — they just lack the mechanism to apply the right fix. Meta-Brain adds that mechanism with minimal parameters.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves X% with ~12M trainable parameters, improving Y% over frozen pi0 baseline and Z% over a parameter-matched rank-48 universal LoRA (~12M), demonstrating that perturbation-diagnostic routing outperforms uniform adaptation at equivalent parameter budget.\"\n\n## Contribution Structure (single system contribution, not 3 independent claims)\n\nOne well-executed systems contribution: Meta-Brain — a modular framework that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA activations and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts. Supporting evidence:\n1. **Empirical finding**: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity (prerequisite observation)\n2. **Architecture + ablation**: PAR router + LoRA bank with comprehensive ablation ladder including parameter-matched controls (rank-48 universal), random routing baseline, and rank scaling analysis\n3. **Practical insight**: Layer placement analysis reveals perturbation-type-to-layer mapping (V→early, S→mid, P→late)\n\nNote: Correction Memory is an optional module — present as ablation footnote, NOT as a named architectural component. Dream reviewers across all tiers viewed CM (+1-2%) as padding.\n\n## Venue Fit (CoRL 2026)\n\n**Strengths**:\n- Pi0 backbone + LIBERO-Plus eval is core CoRL territory\n- Architecture operates on representations (sim evaluation methodologically sufficient)\n- Parameter efficiency matters for real-robot deployment (~12M vs 114M)\n- Single forward pass routing = no latency overhead for real-time control\n\n**Risks**:\n- Sim-only evaluation (mitigated: LIBERO-Plus is CVPR 2026 standard, sim-only is accepted pattern at CoRL for architecture contributions)\n- Single backbone (pi0) — need gain-over-baseline framing to enable contextualized comparison with VLA-GSE (Qwen3-VL-4B)\n- Absolute performance may trail SRPO (82.1%) / CorridorVLA (83.21%) which use full training — frame as parameter-efficient approach, not absolute SOTA chase\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### C1: \"Phase 0 classification is too easy — trivially solvable routing\"\n**Risk**: High Phase 0 accuracy (F1=0.993) may suggest the task is trivial and routing adds no value.\n**Response**: Easy diagnosis ≠ easy correction. The value of routing is in Phase 1 (specialized vs universal LoRA), not Phase 0. Easy routing is actually desirable — it means the router adds zero overhead concern. Ablation exp-005 directly tests whether routing matters.\n\n### C2: \"Sim-only evaluation, no real robot\"\n**Risk**: CoRL values real-world experiments.\n**Response**: LIBERO-Plus is the standard robustness benchmark (CVPR 2026). Meta-Brain operates on representations, not hardware-specific signals. Sim-only is the established evaluation pattern for VLA architecture contributions at CoRL (SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA all sim-only on LIBERO variants).\n\n### C3: \"Single backbone (pi0), limited generalizability\"\n**Risk**: Results may be specific to pi0's PaliGemma architecture.\n**Response**: (a) Phase 0 finding (perturbation classifiability) is grounded in mechanistic evidence across multiple VLAs (SAFE on OpenVLA, probing on OpenVLA, feature steering on pi0.5 + OpenVLA). (b) Tier 3 cross-backbone experiment on SmolVLA/OpenVLA if time permits. (c) Gain-over-baseline metric enables fair comparison across backbones.\n\n### C4: \"Parameter budget comparison unfair — different backbone from VLA-GSE\"\n**Risk**: Comparing 6M (on pi0) vs 114M (on Qwen3-VL-4B) is apples-to-oranges.\n**Response**: Compare gain-over-respective-LoRA-baseline: VLA-GSE gains +12.0% over LoRA (69.2% → 81.2%); Meta-Brain should gain X% over universal LoRA on pi0. Parameter efficiency = gain per trainable parameter. Also report absolute numbers with caveat.\n\n### C5: \"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\"\n**Risk**: Lumping camera+light+background+noise into \"Visual\" may lose useful distinctions.\n**Response**: Phase 0 6-class analysis shows intra-Visual perturbations are more similar to each other than to Spatial/Physical. 3-class matches the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing (early/mid/late layers). Ablation: 6-class routing vs 3-class routing comparison.\n\n### C6: \"Correction Memory adds complexity for marginal gain\"\n**Risk**: CM may seem over-engineered for routing-weight modulation.\n**Response**: CM is presented as an optional module. Ablation (with/without CM) quantifies its contribution. CM adds only 1 learned parameter (alpha) + CPU-bound FAISS.\n\n### C7: \"Unseen perturbation types at deployment — open-set robustness\"\n**Risk**: 3-class taxonomy is fixed. Novel perturbation types (weather, sensor degradation) will be force-classified to nearest known type, potentially routing to wrong expert.\n**Response**: (a) PAR outputs a probability distribution — low-confidence predictions (high entropy) can trigger fallback to universal LoRA or no correction, providing a natural open-set rejection mechanism. (b) The 3-class taxonomy covers the major perturbation axes in robotic manipulation; truly novel perturbations are an explicit limitation we acknowledge. (c) Correction Memory provides partial adaptation — retrieved exemplars from similar but not identical perturbations can modulate routing appropriately.\n\n### C8: \"Training-deployment feature dimension mismatch\"\n**Risk**: Phase 0 classifier trained on episode-averaged activations (~44 steps) but router uses single-step (K=1) features.\n**Response**: K=1 few-shot analysis directly validates this: F1=0.898 overall, layer 6 K=1=1.0. Paper must explicitly state: (a) classifier is trained on episode-averaged features for label efficiency, (b) deployed router uses single-step features, (c) K=1 validation bridges this gap. Phase 1 PAR router will be trained directly on single-step features to eliminate any residual gap.\n\n## Baselines & Comparisons\n\n| Method | Type | Params | Expected Comparison |\n|--------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| Frozen pi0 | Lower bound | 0 | Our improvement over this = headline number |\n| Universal LoRA (rank 16) | Ablation | ~3.7M | Same per-expert capacity, no routing — tests Claim 2 |\n| Meta-Brain (PAR + ALB) | Ours | ~12M | Full system (3x3.7M experts + 0.5M router) |\n| Meta-Brain + CM | Ours (full) | ~12M + 1 | With Correction Memory |\n| Random routing | Ensemble control | ~12M | Random expert assignment — tests if routing matters vs ensemble effect |\n| Oracle routing | Upper bound | ~12M | Perfect routing — gap to Meta-Brain = routing quality |\n| FTM/FLA | Visual-only | 4K-4.7M | Strong on visual, cannot do spatial/physical |\n| VLA-GSE | Capacity routing | 114M | Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), compare gain-over-LoRA |\n| RobustVLA | Adversarial training | Full model | Uniform approach, compare robustness gain |\n\n## Key Figures (Planned)\n\n1. **Architecture diagram**: Frozen pi0 → PAR (layer 6) → soft routing → LoRA-V/S/P bank → corrected output\n2. **Phase 0 evidence**: t-SNE of layer 6 activations colored by perturbation type + confusion matrix\n3. **Main result table**: LIBERO-Plus per-perturbation breakdown (7 categories × methods)\n4. **Ablation ladder**: Bar chart — Frozen → Universal LoRA → Random Routing → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n5. **Parameter efficiency**: Scatter plot — gain-over-baseline vs trainable parameters (Meta-Brain vs VLA-GSE vs FTM vs RobustVLA)\n6. **Routing analysis**: Heatmap of PAR soft routing weights per perturbation type (should be near-diagonal)\n","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Nv3NFp4HPbdVrvDGnUk8M","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Train LoRA-V/S/P (rank 16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers) on type-specific data plus universal LoRA (rank 16) on mixed data. Evaluate each expert in-type and cross-type. Visual experts trained with online augmentation on LIBERO demos; Spatial/Physical experts trained on oracle-collected LIBERO-Plus demos.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"lora_v_on_visual\": 75.5,\n7\t      \"lora_v_on_spatial\": 63.1,\n8\t      \"lora_v_on_physical\": 59.8,\n9\t      \"lora_s_on_visual\": 62.4,\n10\t      \"lora_s_on_spatial\": 69.2,\n11\t      \"lora_s_on_physical\": 58.3,\n12\t      \"lora_p_on_visual\": 57.6,\n13\t      \"lora_p_on_spatial\": 57.2,\n14\t      \"lora_p_on_physical\": 61.0,\n15\t      \"universal_r16_on_visual\": 65.8,\n16\t      \"universal_r16_on_spatial\": 60.2,\n17\t      \"universal_r16_on_physical\": 55.4,\n18\t      \"universal_r16_overall\": 62.1,\n19\t      \"avg_cross_type_mismatch_drop_pp\": 10.8,\n20\t      \"lora_v_cross_drop\": 13.1,\n21\t      \"lora_s_cross_drop\": 9.0,\n22\t      \"lora_p_cross_drop\": 3.6,\n23\t      \"training_converged_epochs_v\": 15,\n24\t      \"training_converged_epochs_s\": 22,\n25\t      \"training_converged_epochs_p\": 30,\n26\t      \"action_mse_val_lora_v\": 0.0031,\n27\t      \"action_mse_val_lora_s\": 0.0039,\n28\t      \"action_mse_val_lora_p\": 0.0054,\n29\t      \"action_mse_val_universal\": 0.0042\n30\t    },\n31\t    \"tables\": [\n32\t      {\n33\t        \"title\": \"Per-expert success rates (rows=expert, cols=evaluation perturbation type)\",\n34\t        \"content\": \"| Expert | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Avg Cross-type | Cross Δ |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|----------------|---------|\\n| LoRA-V | **75.5** | 63.1 | 59.8 | 61.5 | −13.1 |\\n| LoRA-S | 62.4 | **69.2** | 58.3 | 60.4 | −9.0 |\\n| LoRA-P | 57.6 | 57.2 | **61.0** | 57.4 | −3.6 |\\n| Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | — | — |\\n| Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | — | — |\\n\"\n35\t      }\n36\t    ],\n37\t    \"figures\": [],\n38\t    \"analysis\": \"Experts DO specialize, but unevenly. The asymmetry is the interesting finding: Visual and Spatial LoRA show strong type-specific corrections (cross-drops 13pp and 9pp), while Physical LoRA shows minimal specialization (cross-drop 3.6pp). This maps to the mechanistic story: PaliGemma's representation space strongly encodes visual and spatial information (it's a vision-language model), but physical state (robot joint positions, force dynamics) is weakly represented. LoRA-P's modest 61% on-type and low cross-drop suggest that rank-16 representation-level correction is insufficient for physical perturbations — this needs honest discussion in the paper. Universal r16 at 62.1% sets the parameter-unmatched baseline for ablation.\\n\\n\\nLoRA experts learn partially distinct corrections — cross-type drop exists but is asymmetric across types. LoRA-V shows strongest specialization (cross-drop ~13pp) because visual augmentation training creates a tight distribution match: the expert learns to invert specific visual corruptions (brightness shifts, background swaps) that are absent in spatial/physical data, so applying LoRA-V to non-visual inputs actively degrades representations by \\\"correcting\\\" features that weren't corrupted. LoRA-S shows moderate specialization (~9pp cross-drop): layout perturbations alter mid-level spatial relationships that require qualitatively different corrections from visual corruptions, but there is partial overlap because spatial changes also affect visual appearance. LoRA-P is the weak link: only ~3.6pp cross-drop because physical perturbations (robot initial state) primarily affect action trajectories, not visual representations. LoRA-P's correction at the representation level is modest (61% on-type, barely above universal's 55.4%) — the signal for physical correction is weak at PaliGemma layer 6 where representations encode visual/spatial features better than proprioceptive state. Training convergence reflects this: LoRA-V converges in 15 epochs (strong, clean signal), LoRA-P takes 30 epochs with higher validation MSE (noisy, weak signal). The average cross-drop of ~10.8pp is in the median range — specialization exists but is less dramatic than best-case, driven mostly by Visual/Spatial divergence while Physical contributes little.\\n\"\n39\t  },\n40\t  {\n41\t    \"name\": \"Rank-48 parameter-matched universal LoRA — existential control\",\n42\t    \"description\": \"Train single universal LoRA with rank 48 (~12M params) on mixed all-perturbation data. Parameter-matched to Meta-Brain's total budget. THE existential test for Claim 2.\\n\",\n43\t    \"results\": {\n44\t      \"rank48_universal_overall\": 66.4,\n45\t      \"rank48_on_visual\": 70.1,\n46\t      \"rank48_on_spatial\": 64.3,\n47\t      \"rank48_on_physical\": 58.6,\n48\t      \"rank48_vs_rank16_gain_pp\": 4.3,\n49\t      \"rank48_vs_meta_brain_gap_pp\": -5.6,\n50\t      \"claim2_survival\": true,\n51\t      \"training_epochs\": 18,\n52\t      \"action_mse_val\": 0.0036\n53\t    },\n54\t    \"tables\": [\n55\t      {\n56\t        \"title\": \"Rank-48 universal vs Meta-Brain per-type comparison\",\n57\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\\n|--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\\n| Universal r16 | 3.7M | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | 62.1 |\\n| Universal r48 | 12M | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 | 66.4 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\\n| Δ (MB − r48) | 0 | +5.4 | +4.9 | +2.4 | +5.6 |\\n\"\n58\t      }\n59\t    ],\n60\t    \"figures\": [],\n61\t    \"analysis\": \"Claim 2 survives — routing adds 5.6pp over parameter-matched universal. But the per-type decomposition tells the real story: routing earns its keep on Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp) where experts learn genuinely distinct corrections, but adds only +2.4pp on Physical where the expert itself is limited. Paper should frame: \\\"routing is most beneficial for perturbation types that require qualitatively different representation-level corrections; for perturbation types that primarily affect action-space dynamics (Physical), the routing advantage is modest.\\\"\\n\\n\\nRank-48 universal captures most of the low-hanging correction accessible via parameter scaling alone. The +4.3pp gain over rank-16 (62.1→66.4) shows that extra capacity helps uniformly — but with sharply diminishing returns (3.2× the parameters for only 1.4× the gain-over-frozen). This diminishing return is because a single adapter learning mixed-perturbation data must find a compromise correction that partially works for all types but optimally corrects none. The rank-48 adapter effectively learns the average correction direction across perturbation types; it boosts visual most (+4.3pp) because visual perturbations are most common in training data and share the most overlap, while physical gains least (+3.2pp) because physical correction requires a qualitatively different direction. The 66.4% vs Meta-Brain's 72.0% leaves a 5.6pp gap — enough to validate Claim 2 but not dramatic. This gap is the routing premium: what you gain by allocating the same 12M parameters to three specialized experts with informed routing vs one universal adapter. The gap is largest on visual (70.1 vs 75.5, ~5.4pp) where specialization matters most and smallest on physical (58.6 vs 61.0, ~2.4pp) where even specialized LoRA-P barely outperforms.\\n\"\n62\t  },\n63\t  {\n64\t    \"name\": \"Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end\",\n65\t    \"description\": \"Assemble full Meta-Brain system: PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, first-token, 2-layer MLP) produces soft routing weights p(type_k). Corrected hidden state h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA expert parameters. Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\\n\",\n66\t    \"results\": {\n67\t      \"meta_brain_overall\": 72.0,\n68\t      \"meta_brain_visual\": 75.5,\n69\t      \"meta_brain_spatial\": 69.2,\n70\t      \"meta_brain_physical\": 61.0,\n71\t      \"meta_brain_camera\": 78.0,\n72\t      \"meta_brain_light\": 79.2,\n73\t      \"meta_brain_background\": 71.0,\n74\t      \"meta_brain_noise\": 73.6,\n75\t      \"meta_brain_layout\": 69.2,\n76\t      \"meta_brain_robot\": 61.0,\n77\t      \"routing_weight_visual_to_lora_v\": 0.82,\n78\t      \"routing_weight_visual_to_lora_s\": 0.11,\n79\t      \"routing_weight_visual_to_lora_p\": 0.07,\n80\t      \"routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_s\": 0.76,\n81\t      \"routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_v\": 0.14,\n82\t      \"routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_p\": 0.1,\n83\t      \"routing_weight_physical_to_lora_p\": 0.67,\n84\t      \"routing_weight_physical_to_lora_v\": 0.14,\n85\t      \"routing_weight_physical_to_lora_s\": 0.19,\n86\t      \"routing_entropy_visual\": 0.41,\n87\t      \"routing_entropy_spatial\": 0.52,\n88\t      \"routing_entropy_physical\": 0.72,\n89\t      \"gain_over_frozen\": 18.4,\n90\t      \"gain_over_universal_r16\": 9.9,\n91\t      \"gain_over_universal_r48\": 5.6\n92\t    },\n93\t    \"tables\": [\n94\t      {\n95\t        \"title\": \"Meta-Brain per-perturbation success rates\",\n96\t        \"content\": \"| Perturbation | Category | Success (%) | Frozen pi0 (%) | Δ |\\n|-------------|----------|-------------|----------------|------|\\n| Camera jitter | Visual | 78.0 | 55.2 | +22.8 |\\n| Light change | Visual | 79.2 | 60.8 | +18.4 |\\n| Background swap | Visual | 71.0 | 54.0 | +17.0 |\\n| Sensor noise | Visual | 73.6 | 56.4 | +17.2 |\\n| Layout random | Spatial | 69.2 | 51.6 | +17.6 |\\n| Robot state | Physical | 61.0 | 47.2 | +13.8 |\\n| **Overall** | **—** | **72.0** | **53.6** | **+18.4** |\\n\"\n97\t      },\n98\t      {\n99\t        \"title\": \"PAR routing weight distribution (per input category)\",\n100\t        \"content\": \"| Input Category | w(LoRA-V) | w(LoRA-S) | w(LoRA-P) | Entropy |\\n|---------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\\n| Visual | **0.82** | 0.11 | 0.07 | 0.41 |\\n| Spatial | 0.14 | **0.76** | 0.10 | 0.52 |\\n| Physical | 0.14 | 0.19 | **0.67** | 0.72 |\\n\"\n101\t      }\n102\t    ],\n103\t    \"figures\": [],\n104\t    \"analysis\": \"The routing weight heatmap is the key diagnostic figure. The entropy gradient (0.41→0.52→0.72) perfectly anti-correlates with success rate (75.5→69.2→61.0), suggesting routing confidence is a reliable proxy for expected correction quality. This motivates two practical recommendations: (1) confidence-gated routing — fall back to frozen when entropy exceeds a threshold (addresses clean LIBERO degradation), and (2) hard routing at deployment — if soft routing blending dilutes specialization, temperature-sharpened or argmax routing may close part of the oracle gap. The physical category's high entropy and low success rate is the honest limitation: Meta-Brain's representation-level correction has diminishing returns for perturbation types that primarily require action-space adaptation.\\n\\n\\nSoft routing via PAR is accurate but introduces blending that partially dilutes expert specialization — this is the core median-tier mechanism. For visual perturbations, the router confidently assigns ~82% weight to LoRA-V (entropy 0.41) because Phase 0 showed visual perturbations create the most distinctive activation signatures at layer 6. The resulting 75.5% visual accuracy is strong but below the oracle ceiling because 18% of the correction comes from mismatched experts. For spatial, routing is slightly less decisive (76% to LoRA-S, entropy 0.52) because some spatial perturbations (e.g., layout changes that also alter visual appearance) overlap with visual signatures, producing moderate blending and 69.2%. For physical, the router is least confident (67% to LoRA-P, entropy 0.72) — physical perturbation signatures are weakest at PaliGemma layer 6 because this layer primarily encodes visual-spatial features, not proprioceptive state. The high entropy means ~33% of correction comes from LoRA-V/S, which applies visual/spatial corrections to physical inputs — not harmful but not helpful either. Physical at 61.0% is only +2.4pp above rank-48 universal (58.6%), making it the weak link. The overall 72.0% is a solid +18.4pp over frozen pi0 but the per-type gradient (75.5→69.2→61.0) is the interesting story. Routing confidence (entropy) directly predicts per-type success — this correlation supports confidence-gated routing as a practical recommendation.\\n\"\n105\t  },\n106\t  {\n107\t    \"name\": \"Oracle routing ablation — upper bound\",\n108\t    \"description\": \"Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard one-hot routing). Measures the gap between learned soft routing and perfect hard routing — upper bound for routing quality.\\n\",\n109\t    \"results\": {\n110\t      \"oracle_overall\": 77.0,\n111\t      \"oracle_visual\": 81.8,\n112\t      \"oracle_spatial\": 74.6,\n113\t      \"oracle_physical\": 66.4,\n114\t      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_overall\": 5.0,\n115\t      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_visual\": 6.3,\n116\t      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_spatial\": 5.4,\n117\t      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_physical\": 5.4\n118\t    },\n119\t    \"tables\": [\n120\t      {\n121\t        \"title\": \"Oracle vs PAR routing comparison\",\n122\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\\n| Meta-Brain (PAR) | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\\n| Oracle routing | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 | 77.0 |\\n| Δ | +6.3 | +5.4 | +5.4 | +5.0 |\\n\"\n123\t      }\n124\t    ],\n125\t    \"figures\": [],\n126\t    \"analysis\": \"The oracle gap of 5.0pp is moderate — PAR captures most of the routing value (72/77 = 93.5% of oracle performance) but leaves room for improvement. The uniformity of the gap across types suggests that improving routing sharpness (e.g., lower-temperature softmax, curriculum-based routing training) would benefit all categories equally. Present as concrete future work: \\\"hard routing or temperature-annealed routing could recover an estimated 2-3pp of the 5pp oracle gap.\\\" The oracle at 77% also contextualizes the absolute performance ceiling of the 3-expert architecture — further gains require stronger individual experts (higher rank) or additional experts.\\n\\n\\nOracle routing eliminates soft routing blending — each input gets 100% weight on the matched expert. The 5.0pp overall gap over Meta-Brain comes from two compounding effects: (1) eliminating dilution from mismatched expert blending (accounts for ~3pp — the 18-33% off-diagonal mass in PAR routing now goes to zero), and (2) perfect type assignment on ambiguous boundary cases where PAR misroutes (accounts for ~2pp — PAR's 0.996 F1 still makes errors on a few percent of timesteps, and these compound over 44-step episodes). The gap is roughly uniform across types (5.0-6.3pp) rather than concentrated on physical, suggesting the PAR router's limitation is general blending behavior, not systematic failure on any specific type. Oracle physical at 66.4% confirms that even perfect routing cannot push physical above the expert's ceiling — LoRA-P itself is the bottleneck, not the routing. The 77.0% oracle sets a meaningful upper bound: the 3-expert architecture has 5pp more headroom, achievable through sharper routing (temperature annealing, hard routing, or better router architectures).\\n\"\n127\t  },\n128\t  {\n129\t    \"name\": \"Ablation ladder — Frozen → Universal → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle\",\n130\t    \"description\": \"Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal r16, Random routing (3×r16 experts), Universal r48 (parameter-matched), Meta-Brain, Oracle routing. Report overall + per-perturbation breakdown with standard deviations and statistical significance.\\n\",\n131\t    \"results\": {\n132\t      \"frozen_overall\": 53.6,\n133\t      \"frozen_std\": 2.1,\n134\t      \"universal_r16_overall\": 62.1,\n135\t      \"universal_r16_std\": 1.8,\n136\t      \"universal_r48_overall\": 66.4,\n137\t      \"universal_r48_std\": 1.6,\n138\t      \"random_routing_overall\": 64.2,\n139\t      \"random_routing_std\": 2.5,\n140\t      \"meta_brain_overall\": 72.0,\n141\t      \"meta_brain_std\": 1.9,\n142\t      \"oracle_overall\": 77.0,\n143\t      \"oracle_std\": 1.4,\n144\t      \"meta_brain_vs_random_gap\": 7.8,\n145\t      \"meta_brain_vs_r48_gap\": 5.6,\n146\t      \"meta_brain_vs_frozen_gain\": 18.4,\n147\t      \"random_vs_r16_gap\": 2.1,\n148\t      \"random_vs_r48_gap\": -2.2,\n149\t      \"p_value_mb_vs_random\": 0.003,\n150\t      \"p_value_mb_vs_r48\": 0.008,\n151\t      \"p_value_mb_vs_r16\": \"<0.001\"\n152\t    },\n153\t    \"tables\": [\n154\t      {\n155\t        \"title\": \"Ablation ladder — LIBERO-Plus overall success rate (5 seeds)\",\n156\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Overall (%) | Std | Δ vs Frozen | Δ vs r48 |\\n|--------|--------|------------|-----|-------------|----------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 0 | 53.6 | 2.1 | — | −12.8 |\\n| Universal r16 | 3.7M | 62.1 | 1.8 | +8.5 | −4.3 |\\n| Random routing | 12M | 64.2 | 2.5 | +10.6 | −2.2 |\\n| Universal r48 | 12M | 66.4 | 1.6 | +12.8 | — |\\n| **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **72.0** | **1.9** | **+18.4** | **+5.6** |\\n| Oracle routing | 12M | 77.0 | 1.4 | +23.4 | +10.6 |\\n\"\n157\t      },\n158\t      {\n159\t        \"title\": \"Per-perturbation-category breakdown\",\n160\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 |\\n| Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 |\\n| Random routing | 67.8 | 62.4 | 57.0 |\\n| Universal r48 | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 |\\n| Oracle | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 |\\n\"\n161\t      },\n162\t      {\n163\t        \"title\": \"Statistical significance (pairwise p-values, two-sided t-test, 5 seeds)\",\n164\t        \"content\": \"| Comparison | Δ (pp) | p-value | Significant (α=0.01) |\\n|-----------|--------|---------|---------------------|\\n| MB vs Frozen | +18.4 | <0.001 | ✓ |\\n| MB vs r16 | +9.9 | <0.001 | ✓ |\\n| MB vs Random | +7.8 | 0.003 | ✓ |\\n| MB vs r48 | +5.6 | 0.008 | ✓ |\\n| Oracle vs MB | +5.0 | 0.011 | ✗ (marginal) |\\n| Random vs r16 | +2.1 | 0.14 | ✗ |\\n| Random vs r48 | −2.2 | 0.18 | ✗ |\\n\"\n165\t      }\n166\t    ],\n167\t    \"figures\": [],\n168\t    \"analysis\": \"The ablation ladder is the paper's core figure. Critical narrative points: (1) Random < r48 is the surprise result — it proves that routing QUALITY matters, not just expert diversity. Without this, a reviewer could argue Meta-Brain's gains are just ensemble effects. (2) Meta-Brain vs r48 (+5.6pp, p=0.008) is statistically significant but requires careful framing: \\\"modest but reliable routing premium.\\\" (3) The per-type breakdown shows Physical compresses the ladder — all methods are closer together on Physical than on Visual/Spatial. This is the honest story: routing helps most where representation-level corrections differ qualitatively by type. (4) Oracle−MB significance is marginal (p=0.011) — the paper can discuss the oracle gap as suggestive rather than definitive.\\n\\n\\nThe ablation ladder forms a clean monotonic ordering: Frozen (53.6) < r16 (62.1) < Random (64.2) < r48 (66.4) < Meta-Brain (72.0) < Oracle (77.0). Each step isolates a specific factor:\\nFrozen→r16 (+8.5pp): LoRA adaptation on perturbed data helps, even with limited capacity and no specialization. This is the \\\"any correction is better than none\\\" baseline gain.\\nr16→Random (+2.1pp): Random routing gives 1/3 chance of the right expert, plus the ensemble effect of having 3× the parameters (12M vs 3.7M). The modest +2.1pp shows the ensemble effect alone is minor — randomly applying the wrong expert often hurts more than the extra capacity helps.\\nRandom→r48 (−2.2pp): Random routing actually underperforms the parameter-matched universal r48. This is important: 12M parameters concentrated in one coherent adapter (r48) outperform 12M parameters split across 3 randomly-assigned experts. Random routing's 2/3 mismatch penalty exceeds its 1/3 specialization benefit. This directly supports the claim that INFORMED routing is necessary — mere expert diversity without correct assignment is worse than a single universal adapter.\\nr48→Meta-Brain (+5.6pp): The routing premium. Informed routing turns the 3-expert architecture from a liability (random: −2.2pp vs r48) into a significant advantage (+5.6pp vs r48). This is the core evidence for Claim 2.\\nMeta-Brain→Oracle (+5.0pp): Room for routing improvement. The PAR router captures most of the value but soft blending leaves 5pp on the table.\\nStatistical significance: Meta-Brain vs random (p=0.003) and vs r48 (p=0.008) are significant at α=0.01 across 5 seeds. Random routing has the highest variance (std=2.5) due to stochastic expert assignment; oracle has the lowest (std=1.4) due to deterministic correct routing.\\n\"\n169\t  },\n170\t  {\n171\t    \"name\": \"FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline\",\n172\t    \"description\": \"Reproduce FTM on pi0 backbone for visual perturbation types. Compare Meta-Brain's visual performance vs FTM. Establish that Meta-Brain matches visual-only methods on visual while additionally covering spatial/physical.\\n\",\n173\t    \"results\": {\n174\t      \"ftm_visual_overall\": 73.8,\n175\t      \"ftm_camera\": 76.4,\n176\t      \"ftm_light\": 78.2,\n177\t      \"ftm_background\": 67.8,\n178\t      \"ftm_noise\": 72.6,\n179\t      \"ftm_spatial\": 52.4,\n180\t      \"ftm_physical\": 47.8,\n181\t      \"ftm_overall\": 60.8,\n182\t      \"ftm_params_M\": 0.004,\n183\t      \"meta_brain_visual_overall\": 75.5,\n184\t      \"meta_brain_vs_ftm_visual_delta\": 1.7,\n185\t      \"meta_brain_vs_ftm_spatial_delta\": 16.8,\n186\t      \"meta_brain_vs_ftm_physical_delta\": 13.2\n187\t    },\n188\t    \"tables\": [\n189\t      {\n190\t        \"title\": \"FTM vs Meta-Brain comparison\",\n191\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\\n|--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | 53.6 |\\n| FTM | 4K | 73.8 | 52.4 | 47.8 | 60.8 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\\n| Δ (MB − FTM) | — | +1.7 | +16.8 | +13.2 | +11.2 |\\n\"\n192\t      }\n193\t    ],\n194\t    \"figures\": [],\n195\t    \"analysis\": \"FTM comparison reinforces the multi-type robustness narrative. Do NOT claim Meta-Brain beats FTM on visual — the 1.7pp gap is not significant and FTM achieves this with 3000× fewer parameters. Instead frame: \\\"Meta-Brain matches purpose-built visual methods while providing broad perturbation coverage that single-modality approaches cannot. The parameter cost (12M vs 4K) is the price of multi-type generality.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFTM is purpose-built for visual robustness with extreme parameter efficiency (~4K params), so it achieves strong 73.8% on visual perturbations — only 1.7pp below Meta-Brain's 75.5%. This is expected: FTM's fine-tuned visual transforms directly target the same representation corruption that LoRA-V corrects, and 4K purpose-designed parameters can be more efficient than 3.7M generic LoRA parameters for a narrow task. However, FTM on spatial (52.4%) and physical (47.8%) is at or below frozen pi0 level — its visual-only training provides zero spatial/physical correction and occasionally interferes (physical 47.8% vs frozen 47.2% suggests slight degradation from applying visual transforms to non-visual inputs). FTM's background swap result (67.8%) is notably lower than its camera/light/noise results because background swaps introduce novel textures that FTM's limited capacity cannot fully absorb. Meta-Brain's value proposition is clear: comparable visual performance (+1.7pp) plus +16.8pp on spatial and +13.2pp on physical that FTM cannot touch.\\n\"\n196\t  },\n197\t  {\n198\t    \"name\": \"Parameter efficiency analysis\",\n199\t    \"description\": \"Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Generate scatter plot comparing Meta-Brain, Universal variants, FTM, VLA-GSE.\\n\",\n200\t    \"results\": {\n201\t      \"meta_brain_gain_pp\": 18.4,\n202\t      \"meta_brain_params_M\": 12,\n203\t      \"meta_brain_gain_per_Mparam\": 1.53,\n204\t      \"universal_r16_gain_pp\": 8.5,\n205\t      \"universal_r16_params_M\": 3.7,\n206\t      \"universal_r16_gain_per_Mparam\": 2.3,\n207\t      \"universal_r48_gain_pp\": 12.8,\n208\t      \"universal_r48_params_M\": 12,\n209\t      \"universal_r48_gain_per_Mparam\": 1.07,\n210\t      \"ftm_gain_pp_visual_only\": 16.0,\n211\t      \"ftm_params_M\": 0.004,\n212\t      \"ftm_gain_per_Mparam\": 4000.0,\n213\t      \"ftm_gain_pp_overall\": 7.2,\n214\t      \"ftm_gain_per_Mparam_overall\": 1800.0,\n215\t      \"vla_gse_gain_pp\": 27.6,\n216\t      \"vla_gse_params_M\": 114,\n217\t      \"vla_gse_gain_per_Mparam\": 0.24,\n218\t      \"vla_gse_note\": \"different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), indirect comparison only\"\n219\t    },\n220\t    \"tables\": [\n221\t      {\n222\t        \"title\": \"Parameter efficiency comparison\",\n223\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Gain over frozen | Gain/Mparam | Coverage |\\n|--------|--------|-----------------|-------------|----------|\\n| FTM | 4K | +16.0pp (visual) | 4000.0 | Visual only |\\n| Universal r16 | 3.7M | +8.5pp | 2.30 | All (uniform) |\\n| **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **+18.4pp** | **1.53** | **All (specialized)** |\\n| Universal r48 | 12M | +12.8pp | 1.07 | All (uniform) |\\n| VLA-GSE* | 114M | +27.6pp* | 0.24* | All (capacity) |\\n\"\n224\t      }\n225\t    ],\n226\t    \"figures\": [],\n227\t    \"analysis\": \"The scatter plot is the Claim 3 figure. Meta-Brain's position above the universal LoRA scaling line visually demonstrates the routing premium. However, FTM's dominance on visual-only efficiency needs honest treatment — acknowledge that for deployments facing only visual perturbations, FTM is more efficient. Meta-Brain's value is multi-type coverage with routing specialization. VLA-GSE comparison must carry the \\\"different backbone\\\" caveat prominently — apples-to-oranges with asterisk, not a direct parameter efficiency claim.\\n\\n\\nParameter efficiency depends on what you measure. FTM dominates gain-per-param on visual-only (4000 gain/Mparam) because it was designed for exactly this — but its overall gain-per-param (1800) still leads because overall averages include the visual types where FTM excels. Universal r16 has better gain-per-param (2.30) than Meta-Brain (1.53) because smaller models have an efficiency advantage on the low-hanging-fruit gains. The critical comparison is r48 (1.07) vs Meta-Brain (1.53): at the same parameter budget, routing gives 43% better efficiency than uniform adaptation. VLA-GSE at 0.24 gain/Mparam (on a different backbone, hence indirect) shows that Meta-Brain is ~6.4× more parameter-efficient in gain-per-param terms. The scatter plot will show Meta-Brain above the \\\"universal LoRA scaling line\\\" (connecting r16 and r48 points), visually demonstrating that routing adds value beyond what parameter scaling alone provides.\\n\"\n228\t  },\n229\t  {\n230\t    \"name\": \"Correction Memory ablation\",\n231\t    \"description\": \"Compare Meta-Brain ± Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing weights based on FAISS-retrieved exemplars. Quantify marginal contribution.\\n\",\n232\t    \"results\": {\n233\t      \"meta_brain_no_cm_overall\": 72.0,\n234\t      \"meta_brain_with_cm_overall\": 73.2,\n235\t      \"cm_delta_overall\": 1.2,\n236\t      \"cm_delta_visual\": 1.4,\n237\t      \"cm_delta_spatial\": 1.2,\n238\t      \"cm_delta_physical\": 0.8,\n239\t      \"cm_p_value\": 0.12,\n240\t      \"cm_retrieval_latency_ms\": 2.3,\n241\t      \"cm_alpha_learned\": 0.15\n242\t    },\n243\t    \"tables\": [\n244\t      {\n245\t        \"title\": \"Correction Memory ablation\",\n246\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall | p-value |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|---------|\\n| Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 | — |\\n| Meta-Brain + CM | 76.9 | 70.4 | 61.8 | 73.2 | 0.12 |\\n| Δ CM | +1.4 | +1.2 | +0.8 | +1.2 | n.s. |\\n\"\n247\t      }\n248\t    ],\n249\t    \"figures\": [],\n250\t    \"analysis\": \"CM is confirmed as ablation footnote material. Present as: \\\"An optional Correction Memory module provides +1.2pp (n.s.) by modulating routing weights with retrieved exemplars. We include this result for completeness but recommend the CM-free architecture for deployment simplicity.\\\" Do NOT elevate CM in the architecture diagram or contribution list.\\n\\n\\nCM provides a marginal +1.2pp improvement that is not statistically significant (p=0.12 across 5 seeds). The learned alpha=0.15 means CM modulates routing weights by only 15% at most — the base PAR router already does most of the work. The per-type pattern follows the same gradient as all other results: visual benefits slightly more (+1.4pp) because visual perturbation exemplars are most distinctive in representation space and FAISS retrieval is most accurate. Physical benefits least (+0.8pp) because physical perturbation representations are less separable, so retrieved exemplars are noisier. The 2.3ms retrieval latency is negligible but the architectural complexity (FAISS index, exemplar storage, alpha parameter) is not justified by 1.2pp. Dream reviewers unanimously flagged CM as padding — this ablation confirms that assessment.\\n\"\n251\t  },\n252\t  {\n253\t    \"name\": \"Router architecture comparison\",\n254\t    \"description\": \"Compare PAR router variants: 2-layer MLP (default), linear classifier, attention- based router. All on PaliGemma layer 6, first-token pooling.\\n\",\n255\t    \"results\": {\n256\t      \"linear_routing_f1\": 0.988,\n257\t      \"linear_downstream_success\": 70.6,\n258\t      \"linear_params_M\": 0.1,\n259\t      \"mlp_routing_f1\": 0.996,\n260\t      \"mlp_downstream_success\": 72.0,\n261\t      \"mlp_params_M\": 0.5,\n262\t      \"attention_routing_f1\": 0.997,\n263\t      \"attention_downstream_success\": 72.4,\n264\t      \"attention_params_M\": 0.8\n265\t    },\n266\t    \"tables\": [\n267\t      {\n268\t        \"title\": \"Router architecture comparison\",\n269\t        \"content\": \"| Router | Params | Routing F1 | Downstream (%) | Δ vs MLP |\\n|--------|--------|-----------|----------------|----------|\\n| Linear | 0.1M | 0.988 | 70.6 | −1.4 |\\n| 2-layer MLP | 0.5M | 0.996 | 72.0 | — |\\n| Attention | 0.8M | 0.997 | 72.4 | +0.4 |\\n\"\n270\t      }\n271\t    ],\n272\t    \"figures\": [],\n273\t    \"analysis\": \"Router architecture is not a bottleneck. Present as a single sentence in the ablation section: \\\"We compared linear, MLP, and attention-based routers; the 2-layer MLP provides the best complexity-performance tradeoff (Table N).\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe routing classification task is nearly saturated (Phase 0 F1=0.993), so router architecture differences are small. Linear classifier's slight accuracy gap (F1 0.988 vs 0.996) translates to ~1.4pp downstream loss: the 0.8% additional routing errors occur on ambiguous boundary cases (e.g., background swaps that also affect spatial layout), and these errors compound over multi-step rollouts (a single misroute early in an episode can cascade). Attention-based router marginally improves (F1 0.997, +0.4pp downstream) by better handling these boundary cases through cross-dimensional attention on the 2048-dim PaliGemma features, but the gain doesn't justify 60% more parameters. The 2-layer MLP is Pareto-optimal: near-perfect routing at minimal overhead.\\n\"\n274\t  },\n275\t  {\n276\t    \"name\": \"6-class vs 3-class routing\",\n277\t    \"description\": \"Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot, 6 LoRA experts) vs 3-class coarse routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts).\\n\",\n278\t    \"results\": {\n279\t      \"class3_overall\": 72.0,\n280\t      \"class3_params_M\": 12,\n281\t      \"class6_overall\": 72.6,\n282\t      \"class6_params_M\": 24,\n283\t      \"marginal_gain_per_expert\": 0.2,\n284\t      \"class6_camera\": 79.3,\n285\t      \"class6_light\": 80.0,\n286\t      \"class6_background\": 72.8,\n287\t      \"class6_noise\": 75.0,\n288\t      \"class6_layout\": 69.6,\n289\t      \"class6_robot\": 61.4\n290\t    },\n291\t    \"tables\": [\n292\t      {\n293\t        \"title\": \"6-class vs 3-class routing\",\n294\t        \"content\": \"| Routing | Experts | Params | Overall | Δ vs 3-class |\\n|---------|---------|--------|---------|-------------|\\n| 3-class | 3 | 12M | 72.0 | — |\\n| 6-class | 6 | 24M | 72.6 | +0.6 |\\n\"\n295\t      }\n296\t    ],\n297\t    \"figures\": [],\n298\t    \"analysis\": \"Directly addresses reviewer concern C5. 3-class is sufficient — 6-class doubles parameters for 0.6pp. Background swap is the only subtype that meaningfully benefits from its own expert, but not enough to justify the complexity. If a reviewer pushes, point to Phase 0 intra-Visual clustering as the mechanistic justification for grouping.\\n\\n\\n6-class routing with 6 experts provides only +0.6pp over 3-class at 2× the parameters. This is expected from Phase 0: intra-Visual subtypes cluster tightly in activation space (6-class accuracy 99.82% shows they're separable, but 3-class accuracy 99.46% shows the grouping is already natural). The marginal gain per additional expert (0.2pp) is negligible. Within visual subtypes, background swap benefits most from its own expert (+1.8pp: 72.8 vs 71.0) because background textures are the most visually distinct subtype, but camera/light/noise show <1pp gains over being grouped. Spatial and physical are already singleton categories so gain is only from having dedicated visual subtype experts freeing up minor capacity. The 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing (early/mid/late layers) and is the right granularity.\\n\"\n299\t  },\n300\t  {\n301\t    \"name\": \"LoRA rank sweep (r=16/32/64) — capacity-routing interaction\",\n302\t    \"description\": \"Test whether routing benefit grows with expert capacity. At each rank R, compare 3 type-specific rank-R experts with PAR routing vs parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank 3R). Measures capacity-routing interaction.\\n\",\n303\t    \"results\": {\n304\t      \"r16_routed_overall\": 72.0,\n305\t      \"r16_universal_matched_overall\": 66.4,\n306\t      \"r16_routing_benefit\": 5.6,\n307\t      \"r32_routed_overall\": 75.4,\n308\t      \"r32_universal_matched_overall\": 68.6,\n309\t      \"r32_routing_benefit\": 6.8,\n310\t      \"r64_routed_overall\": 78.2,\n311\t      \"r64_universal_matched_overall\": 70.4,\n312\t      \"r64_routing_benefit\": 7.8,\n313\t      \"capacity_threshold\": \"r≥32\",\n314\t      \"r32_total_params_M\": 24,\n315\t      \"r64_total_params_M\": 48\n316\t    },\n317\t    \"tables\": [\n318\t      {\n319\t        \"title\": \"Rank sweep: routing benefit vs expert capacity\",\n320\t        \"content\": \"| Expert Rank | 3×Expert (Routed) | Universal (Matched) | Routing Δ | Total Params |\\n|-------------|-------------------|--------------------|-----------|----|\\n| r=16 | 72.0% | 66.4% | +5.6pp | 12M |\\n| r=32 | 75.4% | 68.6% | +6.8pp | 24M |\\n| r=64 | 78.2% | 70.4% | +7.8pp | 48M |\\n\"\n321\t      }\n322\t    ],\n323\t    \"figures\": [],\n324\t    \"analysis\": \"The rank sweep adds a concrete finding to the paper: \\\"routing benefit scales with expert capacity, with an inflection at r≥32.\\\" At our operating point (r16), routing benefit is 5.6pp — present as the minimum viable routing advantage. The implication: practitioners with larger compute budgets should increase expert rank to maximize the routing premium. The r64 routed result (78.2%) exceeding the r16 oracle (77.0%) is a nice finding — higher-capacity experts + learned routing can surpass perfect-routing of lower-capacity experts. Frame as: \\\"capacity and routing are complementary dimensions of adaptation.\\\"\\n\\n\\nRouting benefit is capacity-dependent, growing from 5.6pp (r16) to 6.8pp (r32) to 7.8pp (r64). The mechanism: at low rank (r16), each expert has limited capacity to specialize — the rank-16 LoRA subspace is constrained enough that specialized and universal corrections partially overlap (both learn \\\"general robustness\\\" corrections that consume most of the limited rank budget, leaving little room for type-specific corrections). At rank 32, experts have enough capacity to learn the general correction AND type-specific refinements — this is where specialization becomes meaningfully different from universality, producing the inflection from 5.6→6.8pp. At rank 64, further capacity allows even finer specialization but with diminishing marginal routing benefit (6.8→7.8pp, only +1.0pp more). The universal LoRA scaling line (66.4→68.6→70.4) shows steady but decelerating gains from rank alone, while the routed line (72.0→75.4→78.2) scales faster because additional rank is leveraged differently by each expert. At r64, the routed system (78.2%) approaches the rank-16 oracle level (77.0%), suggesting that higher-capacity experts can partially compensate for imperfect routing. The capacity threshold of r≥32 is a concrete, actionable finding: \\\"routing requires a minimum expert capacity to be beneficial beyond parameter scaling.\\\"\\n\"\n325\t  },\n326\t  {\n327\t    \"name\": \"Clean LIBERO evaluation — nominal performance preservation\",\n328\t    \"description\": \"Evaluate Meta-Brain on standard (unperturbed) LIBERO benchmark. Verify that routing does not significantly degrade nominal performance.\\n\",\n329\t    \"results\": {\n330\t      \"frozen_pi0_clean_libero\": 92.4,\n331\t      \"meta_brain_clean_libero\": 90.4,\n332\t      \"clean_delta_pp\": -2.0,\n333\t      \"universal_r16_clean_libero\": 91.6,\n334\t      \"universal_r48_clean_libero\": 91.2,\n335\t      \"oracle_clean_libero\": 91.0,\n336\t      \"par_entropy_on_clean\": 0.88,\n337\t      \"par_max_weight_on_clean\": 0.48,\n338\t      \"par_routing_distribution_clean\": \"roughly uniform (0.35, 0.31, 0.34)\"\n339\t    },\n340\t    \"tables\": [\n341\t      {\n342\t        \"title\": \"Clean LIBERO performance\",\n343\t        \"content\": \"| Method | Clean LIBERO (%) | Δ vs Frozen |\\n|--------|-----------------|-------------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 92.4 | — |\\n| Universal r16 | 91.6 | −0.8 |\\n| Universal r48 | 91.2 | −1.2 |\\n| Oracle routing | 91.0 | −1.4 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 90.4 | −2.0 |\\n\"\n344\t      },\n345\t      {\n346\t        \"title\": \"PAR router behavior on clean inputs\",\n347\t        \"content\": \"| Metric | Value |\\n|--------|-------|\\n| Avg entropy | 0.88 |\\n| Max expert weight | 0.48 |\\n| w(LoRA-V) | 0.35 |\\n| w(LoRA-S) | 0.31 |\\n| w(LoRA-P) | 0.34 |\\n\"\n348\t      }\n349\t    ],\n350\t    \"figures\": [],\n351\t    \"analysis\": \"The -2.0pp clean degradation is notable but manageable. The entropy analysis provides a clean solution: confidence-gated routing. Add to the paper as a practical recommendation: \\\"For deployment with mixed clean/perturbed inputs, we recommend entropy-gated routing — suppress corrections when PAR entropy exceeds 0.8 (indicating no clear perturbation type). This recovers frozen performance on clean inputs while preserving routing benefits on perturbed inputs.\\\" Include the entropy histogram as evidence that the gating threshold is well-defined (bimodal distribution with clear separation).\\n\\n\\nMeta-Brain shows -2.0pp degradation on clean LIBERO (90.4% vs frozen 92.4%). The mechanism: on clean inputs, no perturbation correction is needed, but the PAR router still activates with non-trivial confidence. PAR entropy on clean inputs is high (0.88 — near maximum entropy of ln(3)=1.10) and the routing distribution is roughly uniform (0.35/0.31/0.34), meaning the router is uncertain about which type to route to (correct behavior — there IS no perturbation type). However, the near-uniform routing still applies a weighted average of all three expert corrections (each contributing ~33%), introducing small representation shifts that are not correcting any actual perturbation. These spurious corrections occasionally push the policy off its optimal trajectory, causing -2.0pp degradation. Universal LoRA variants show smaller degradation (-0.8pp for r16, -1.2pp for r48) because their single correction direction is less disruptive when unnecessary. Oracle routing on clean inputs (assigning a \\\"clean\\\" label that applies no correction) would be 92.4% by definition, but since oracle routing in practice assigns perturbation types (not a \\\"clean\\\" class), oracle on clean inputs must force-assign a type, yielding 91.0% (-1.4pp). This motivates confidence-gated routing: if PAR entropy exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.8), suppress expert corrections and fall back to frozen representations.\\n\"\n352\t  },\n353\t  {\n354\t    \"name\": \"Cross-backbone validation — OpenVLA\",\n355\t    \"description\": \"Replicate Phase 0 (perturbation classification) + core Phase 1 pipeline on OpenVLA to test generalizability of perturbation-diagnostic routing across architectures.\\n\",\n356\t    \"results\": {\n357\t      \"openvla_phase0_3class_f1\": 0.94,\n358\t      \"openvla_phase0_best_layer\": \"layer_8\",\n359\t      \"openvla_phase0_k1_f1\": 0.86,\n360\t      \"openvla_frozen_overall\": 48.2,\n361\t      \"openvla_universal_r16_overall\": 55.8,\n362\t      \"openvla_meta_brain_overall\": 61.4,\n363\t      \"openvla_routing_benefit\": 5.6,\n364\t      \"openvla_gain_over_frozen\": 13.2\n365\t    },\n366\t    \"tables\": [\n367\t      {\n368\t        \"title\": \"Cross-backbone comparison\",\n369\t        \"content\": \"| Metric | pi0 (PaliGemma) | OpenVLA (Llama) |\\n|--------|-----------------|-----------------|\\n| Phase 0 3-class F1 | 0.993 | 0.94 |\\n| Phase 0 K=1 F1 | 0.898 | 0.86 |\\n| Best classification layer | 6 | 8 |\\n| Frozen baseline | 53.6% | 48.2% |\\n| Universal r16 | 62.1% | 55.8% |\\n| Meta-Brain | 72.0% | 61.4% |\\n| Routing Δ (vs universal r16) | +9.9pp | +5.6pp |\\n\"\n370\t      }\n371\t    ],\n372\t    \"figures\": [],\n373\t    \"analysis\": \"Cross-backbone results support generalizability with caveats. The routing principle works on OpenVLA but with lower absolute numbers and less clean separation. Present as: \\\"perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes across VLA architectures — the routing benefit (5-6pp over universal LoRA) is consistent despite different backbone feature characteristics.\\\" Honestly note that OpenVLA's weaker Phase 0 classification (F1=0.94) sets a lower ceiling for routing quality. This is a P2 experiment — if time is short, Phase 0 replication alone is sufficient for a generalizability claim.\\n\\n\\nPhase 0 partially replicates on OpenVLA: 3-class F1=0.94 is lower than pi0's 0.993 because OpenVLA's Llama backbone produces less cleanly separable perturbation representations than PaliGemma (Llama processes visual tokens through more language-oriented layers, partially mixing perturbation signals). Best layer shifts from layer 6 (pi0/PaliGemma) to layer 8 (OpenVLA/Llama), consistent with different architecture depths. K=1 F1 drops more sharply (0.86 vs 0.898) because single-step features are noisier in OpenVLA. Phase 1 routing benefit on OpenVLA (~5.6pp over universal r16) is comparable to pi0's 5.6pp over r48, suggesting the routing principle generalizes even if absolute performance differs (OpenVLA's frozen baseline is lower at 48.2%). The similar routing benefit across backbones is the key generalizability result — perturbation- diagnostic routing is not pi0-specific.\\n\"\n374\t  },\n375\t  {\n376\t    \"name\": \"LoRA layer placement ablation\",\n377\t    \"description\": \"Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers (default). Test per-expert-type layer placement hypothesis.\\n\",\n378\t    \"results\": {\n379\t      \"lora_v_early_visual\": 76.8,\n380\t      \"lora_v_mid_visual\": 73.2,\n381\t      \"lora_v_late_visual\": 70.4,\n382\t      \"lora_v_all_visual\": 75.5,\n383\t      \"lora_s_early_spatial\": 64.6,\n384\t      \"lora_s_mid_spatial\": 70.8,\n385\t      \"lora_s_late_spatial\": 66.2,\n386\t      \"lora_s_all_spatial\": 69.2,\n387\t      \"lora_p_early_physical\": 57.4,\n388\t      \"lora_p_mid_physical\": 59.2,\n389\t      \"lora_p_late_physical\": 60.8,\n390\t      \"lora_p_all_physical\": 61.0,\n391\t      \"optimal_v\": \"early (2-6)\",\n392\t      \"optimal_s\": \"mid (6-12)\",\n393\t      \"optimal_p\": \"late (12-16) ≈ all layers\"\n394\t    },\n395\t    \"tables\": [\n396\t      {\n397\t        \"title\": \"Layer placement ablation (per-type success rate with matched expert)\",\n398\t        \"content\": \"| Expert | Early (2-6) | Mid (6-12) | Late (12-16) | All (default) | Best |\\n|--------|-------------|------------|-------------|---------------|------|\\n| LoRA-V on Visual | **76.8** | 73.2 | 70.4 | 75.5 | Early |\\n| LoRA-S on Spatial | 64.6 | **70.8** | 66.2 | 69.2 | Mid |\\n| LoRA-P on Physical | 57.4 | 59.2 | 60.8 | **61.0** | All ≈ Late |\\n\"\n399\t      }\n400\t    ],\n401\t    \"figures\": [],\n402\t    \"analysis\": \"Layer placement provides a practical insight for the paper's \\\"layer-perturbation mapping\\\" narrative. V→early and S→mid are clear wins that align with VLA processing stages. P→late/all is ambiguous, reinforcing that physical perturbation correction at the representation level is fundamentally limited. Present as: \\\"perturbation-type-to-layer mapping reveals that visual perturbations are best corrected at early layers (pre-spatial-reasoning) and spatial perturbations at mid layers (spatial-reasoning stage), suggesting type-aware layer assignment as a parameter efficiency optimization.\\\" Note: all-layer (default) is competitive for all types, so the default config is a reasonable choice if practitioners don't want to tune layer assignment.\\n\\nLayer placement shows a partial match to the V→early, S→mid, P→late hypothesis, but with important nuances. LoRA-V is best at early layers (76.8%) because visual perturbations corrupt early representations most — intervening at layers 2-6 corrects corrupted visual features before they propagate to higher layers. LoRA-V at all layers (75.5%) is slightly worse because later LoRA modules introduce unnecessary corrections at layers where visual features are already clean. LoRA-S is best at mid layers (70.8%) because spatial perturbations (layout changes) affect mid-level spatial reasoning where object relationships are encoded — early layers still process raw visual features correctly, and late layers have already committed to action plans. LoRA-P shows the weakest layer preference: late layers (60.8%) are marginally best but barely exceed all layers (61.0%), confirming that physical perturbation correction is weak throughout the representation hierarchy. The lack of strong layer preference for LoRA-P reinforces the median-tier narrative that physical perturbations are poorly addressed at the representation level. The layer placement analysis provides a concrete practical insight: type-specific layer assignment (V→early, S→mid) could improve parameter efficiency by 40% (5 layers × 3 experts = 15 LoRA modules vs 18 layers × 3 experts = 54 modules in the default config).\\n\"\n403\t  }\n404\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90281699-6fb6-4e6d-b094-53549aa733d5","uuid":"29484123-3d6e-41a3-ba01-a824c7d10efe","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:01:00.637Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T165902/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Train LoRA-V/S/P (rank 16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers) on type-specific data plus universal LoRA (rank 16) on mixed data. Evaluate each expert in-type and cross-type. Visual experts trained with online augmentation on LIBERO demos; Spatial/Physical experts trained on oracle-collected LIBERO-Plus demos.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"lora_v_on_visual\": 75.5,\n      \"lora_v_on_spatial\": 63.1,\n      \"lora_v_on_physical\": 59.8,\n      \"lora_s_on_visual\": 62.4,\n      \"lora_s_on_spatial\": 69.2,\n      \"lora_s_on_physical\": 58.3,\n      \"lora_p_on_visual\": 57.6,\n      \"lora_p_on_spatial\": 57.2,\n      \"lora_p_on_physical\": 61.0,\n      \"universal_r16_on_visual\": 65.8,\n      \"universal_r16_on_spatial\": 60.2,\n      \"universal_r16_on_physical\": 55.4,\n      \"universal_r16_overall\": 62.1,\n      \"avg_cross_type_mismatch_drop_pp\": 10.8,\n      \"lora_v_cross_drop\": 13.1,\n      \"lora_s_cross_drop\": 9.0,\n      \"lora_p_cross_drop\": 3.6,\n      \"training_converged_epochs_v\": 15,\n      \"training_converged_epochs_s\": 22,\n      \"training_converged_epochs_p\": 30,\n      \"action_mse_val_lora_v\": 0.0031,\n      \"action_mse_val_lora_s\": 0.0039,\n      \"action_mse_val_lora_p\": 0.0054,\n      \"action_mse_val_universal\": 0.0042\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Per-expert success rates (rows=expert, cols=evaluation perturbation type)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Expert | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Avg Cross-type | Cross Δ |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|----------------|---------|\\n| LoRA-V | **75.5** | 63.1 | 59.8 | 61.5 | −13.1 |\\n| LoRA-S | 62.4 | **69.2** | 58.3 | 60.4 | −9.0 |\\n| LoRA-P | 57.6 | 57.2 | **61.0** | 57.4 | −3.6 |\\n| Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | — | — |\\n| Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | — | — |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Experts DO specialize, but unevenly. The asymmetry is the interesting finding: Visual and Spatial LoRA show strong type-specific corrections (cross-drops 13pp and 9pp), while Physical LoRA shows minimal specialization (cross-drop 3.6pp). This maps to the mechanistic story: PaliGemma's representation space strongly encodes visual and spatial information (it's a vision-language model), but physical state (robot joint positions, force dynamics) is weakly represented. LoRA-P's modest 61% on-type and low cross-drop suggest that rank-16 representation-level correction is insufficient for physical perturbations — this needs honest discussion in the paper. Universal r16 at 62.1% sets the parameter-unmatched baseline for ablation.\\n\\n\\nLoRA experts learn partially distinct corrections — cross-type drop exists but is asymmetric across types. LoRA-V shows strongest specialization (cross-drop ~13pp) because visual augmentation training creates a tight distribution match: the expert learns to invert specific visual corruptions (brightness shifts, background swaps) that are absent in spatial/physical data, so applying LoRA-V to non-visual inputs actively degrades representations by \\\"correcting\\\" features that weren't corrupted. LoRA-S shows moderate specialization (~9pp cross-drop): layout perturbations alter mid-level spatial relationships that require qualitatively different corrections from visual corruptions, but there is partial overlap because spatial changes also affect visual appearance. LoRA-P is the weak link: only ~3.6pp cross-drop because physical perturbations (robot initial state) primarily affect action trajectories, not visual representations. LoRA-P's correction at the representation level is modest (61% on-type, barely above universal's 55.4%) — the signal for physical correction is weak at PaliGemma layer 6 where representations encode visual/spatial features better than proprioceptive state. Training convergence reflects this: LoRA-V converges in 15 epochs (strong, clean signal), LoRA-P takes 30 epochs with higher validation MSE (noisy, weak signal). The average cross-drop of ~10.8pp is in the median range — specialization exists but is less dramatic than best-case, driven mostly by Visual/Spatial divergence while Physical contributes little.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Rank-48 parameter-matched universal LoRA — existential control\",\n    \"description\": \"Train single universal LoRA with rank 48 (~12M params) on mixed all-perturbation data. Parameter-matched to Meta-Brain's total budget. THE existential test for Claim 2.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"rank48_universal_overall\": 66.4,\n      \"rank48_on_visual\": 70.1,\n      \"rank48_on_spatial\": 64.3,\n      \"rank48_on_physical\": 58.6,\n      \"rank48_vs_rank16_gain_pp\": 4.3,\n      \"rank48_vs_meta_brain_gap_pp\": -5.6,\n      \"claim2_survival\": true,\n      \"training_epochs\": 18,\n      \"action_mse_val\": 0.0036\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Rank-48 universal vs Meta-Brain per-type comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\\n|--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\\n| Universal r16 | 3.7M | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | 62.1 |\\n| Universal r48 | 12M | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 | 66.4 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\\n| Δ (MB − r48) | 0 | +5.4 | +4.9 | +2.4 | +5.6 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Claim 2 survives — routing adds 5.6pp over parameter-matched universal. But the per-type decomposition tells the real story: routing earns its keep on Visual (+5.4pp) and Spatial (+4.9pp) where experts learn genuinely distinct corrections, but adds only +2.4pp on Physical where the expert itself is limited. Paper should frame: \\\"routing is most beneficial for perturbation types that require qualitatively different representation-level corrections; for perturbation types that primarily affect action-space dynamics (Physical), the routing advantage is modest.\\\"\\n\\n\\nRank-48 universal captures most of the low-hanging correction accessible via parameter scaling alone. The +4.3pp gain over rank-16 (62.1→66.4) shows that extra capacity helps uniformly — but with sharply diminishing returns (3.2× the parameters for only 1.4× the gain-over-frozen). This diminishing return is because a single adapter learning mixed-perturbation data must find a compromise correction that partially works for all types but optimally corrects none. The rank-48 adapter effectively learns the average correction direction across perturbation types; it boosts visual most (+4.3pp) because visual perturbations are most common in training data and share the most overlap, while physical gains least (+3.2pp) because physical correction requires a qualitatively different direction. The 66.4% vs Meta-Brain's 72.0% leaves a 5.6pp gap — enough to validate Claim 2 but not dramatic. This gap is the routing premium: what you gain by allocating the same 12M parameters to three specialized experts with informed routing vs one universal adapter. The gap is largest on visual (70.1 vs 75.5, ~5.4pp) where specialization matters most and smallest on physical (58.6 vs 61.0, ~2.4pp) where even specialized LoRA-P barely outperforms.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end\",\n    \"description\": \"Assemble full Meta-Brain system: PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, first-token, 2-layer MLP) produces soft routing weights p(type_k). Corrected hidden state h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA expert parameters. Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"meta_brain_overall\": 72.0,\n      \"meta_brain_visual\": 75.5,\n      \"meta_brain_spatial\": 69.2,\n      \"meta_brain_physical\": 61.0,\n      \"meta_brain_camera\": 78.0,\n      \"meta_brain_light\": 79.2,\n      \"meta_brain_background\": 71.0,\n      \"meta_brain_noise\": 73.6,\n      \"meta_brain_layout\": 69.2,\n      \"meta_brain_robot\": 61.0,\n      \"routing_weight_visual_to_lora_v\": 0.82,\n      \"routing_weight_visual_to_lora_s\": 0.11,\n      \"routing_weight_visual_to_lora_p\": 0.07,\n      \"routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_s\": 0.76,\n      \"routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_v\": 0.14,\n      \"routing_weight_spatial_to_lora_p\": 0.1,\n      \"routing_weight_physical_to_lora_p\": 0.67,\n      \"routing_weight_physical_to_lora_v\": 0.14,\n      \"routing_weight_physical_to_lora_s\": 0.19,\n      \"routing_entropy_visual\": 0.41,\n      \"routing_entropy_spatial\": 0.52,\n      \"routing_entropy_physical\": 0.72,\n      \"gain_over_frozen\": 18.4,\n      \"gain_over_universal_r16\": 9.9,\n      \"gain_over_universal_r48\": 5.6\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Meta-Brain per-perturbation success rates\",\n        \"content\": \"| Perturbation | Category | Success (%) | Frozen pi0 (%) | Δ |\\n|-------------|----------|-------------|----------------|------|\\n| Camera jitter | Visual | 78.0 | 55.2 | +22.8 |\\n| Light change | Visual | 79.2 | 60.8 | +18.4 |\\n| Background swap | Visual | 71.0 | 54.0 | +17.0 |\\n| Sensor noise | Visual | 73.6 | 56.4 | +17.2 |\\n| Layout random | Spatial | 69.2 | 51.6 | +17.6 |\\n| Robot state | Physical | 61.0 | 47.2 | +13.8 |\\n| **Overall** | **—** | **72.0** | **53.6** | **+18.4** |\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"PAR routing weight distribution (per input category)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Input Category | w(LoRA-V) | w(LoRA-S) | w(LoRA-P) | Entropy |\\n|---------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\\n| Visual | **0.82** | 0.11 | 0.07 | 0.41 |\\n| Spatial | 0.14 | **0.76** | 0.10 | 0.52 |\\n| Physical | 0.14 | 0.19 | **0.67** | 0.72 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The routing weight heatmap is the key diagnostic figure. The entropy gradient (0.41→0.52→0.72) perfectly anti-correlates with success rate (75.5→69.2→61.0), suggesting routing confidence is a reliable proxy for expected correction quality. This motivates two practical recommendations: (1) confidence-gated routing — fall back to frozen when entropy exceeds a threshold (addresses clean LIBERO degradation), and (2) hard routing at deployment — if soft routing blending dilutes specialization, temperature-sharpened or argmax routing may close part of the oracle gap. The physical category's high entropy and low success rate is the honest limitation: Meta-Brain's representation-level correction has diminishing returns for perturbation types that primarily require action-space adaptation.\\n\\n\\nSoft routing via PAR is accurate but introduces blending that partially dilutes expert specialization — this is the core median-tier mechanism. For visual perturbations, the router confidently assigns ~82% weight to LoRA-V (entropy 0.41) because Phase 0 showed visual perturbations create the most distinctive activation signatures at layer 6. The resulting 75.5% visual accuracy is strong but below the oracle ceiling because 18% of the correction comes from mismatched experts. For spatial, routing is slightly less decisive (76% to LoRA-S, entropy 0.52) because some spatial perturbations (e.g., layout changes that also alter visual appearance) overlap with visual signatures, producing moderate blending and 69.2%. For physical, the router is least confident (67% to LoRA-P, entropy 0.72) — physical perturbation signatures are weakest at PaliGemma layer 6 because this layer primarily encodes visual-spatial features, not proprioceptive state. The high entropy means ~33% of correction comes from LoRA-V/S, which applies visual/spatial corrections to physical inputs — not harmful but not helpful either. Physical at 61.0% is only +2.4pp above rank-48 universal (58.6%), making it the weak link. The overall 72.0% is a solid +18.4pp over frozen pi0 but the per-type gradient (75.5→69.2→61.0) is the interesting story. Routing confidence (entropy) directly predicts per-type success — this correlation supports confidence-gated routing as a practical recommendation.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle routing ablation — upper bound\",\n    \"description\": \"Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard one-hot routing). Measures the gap between learned soft routing and perfect hard routing — upper bound for routing quality.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"oracle_overall\": 77.0,\n      \"oracle_visual\": 81.8,\n      \"oracle_spatial\": 74.6,\n      \"oracle_physical\": 66.4,\n      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_overall\": 5.0,\n      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_visual\": 6.3,\n      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_spatial\": 5.4,\n      \"oracle_minus_meta_brain_physical\": 5.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Oracle vs PAR routing comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\\n| Meta-Brain (PAR) | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\\n| Oracle routing | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 | 77.0 |\\n| Δ | +6.3 | +5.4 | +5.4 | +5.0 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The oracle gap of 5.0pp is moderate — PAR captures most of the routing value (72/77 = 93.5% of oracle performance) but leaves room for improvement. The uniformity of the gap across types suggests that improving routing sharpness (e.g., lower-temperature softmax, curriculum-based routing training) would benefit all categories equally. Present as concrete future work: \\\"hard routing or temperature-annealed routing could recover an estimated 2-3pp of the 5pp oracle gap.\\\" The oracle at 77% also contextualizes the absolute performance ceiling of the 3-expert architecture — further gains require stronger individual experts (higher rank) or additional experts.\\n\\n\\nOracle routing eliminates soft routing blending — each input gets 100% weight on the matched expert. The 5.0pp overall gap over Meta-Brain comes from two compounding effects: (1) eliminating dilution from mismatched expert blending (accounts for ~3pp — the 18-33% off-diagonal mass in PAR routing now goes to zero), and (2) perfect type assignment on ambiguous boundary cases where PAR misroutes (accounts for ~2pp — PAR's 0.996 F1 still makes errors on a few percent of timesteps, and these compound over 44-step episodes). The gap is roughly uniform across types (5.0-6.3pp) rather than concentrated on physical, suggesting the PAR router's limitation is general blending behavior, not systematic failure on any specific type. Oracle physical at 66.4% confirms that even perfect routing cannot push physical above the expert's ceiling — LoRA-P itself is the bottleneck, not the routing. The 77.0% oracle sets a meaningful upper bound: the 3-expert architecture has 5pp more headroom, achievable through sharper routing (temperature annealing, hard routing, or better router architectures).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation ladder — Frozen → Universal → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle\",\n    \"description\": \"Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal r16, Random routing (3×r16 experts), Universal r48 (parameter-matched), Meta-Brain, Oracle routing. Report overall + per-perturbation breakdown with standard deviations and statistical significance.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"frozen_overall\": 53.6,\n      \"frozen_std\": 2.1,\n      \"universal_r16_overall\": 62.1,\n      \"universal_r16_std\": 1.8,\n      \"universal_r48_overall\": 66.4,\n      \"universal_r48_std\": 1.6,\n      \"random_routing_overall\": 64.2,\n      \"random_routing_std\": 2.5,\n      \"meta_brain_overall\": 72.0,\n      \"meta_brain_std\": 1.9,\n      \"oracle_overall\": 77.0,\n      \"oracle_std\": 1.4,\n      \"meta_brain_vs_random_gap\": 7.8,\n      \"meta_brain_vs_r48_gap\": 5.6,\n      \"meta_brain_vs_frozen_gain\": 18.4,\n      \"random_vs_r16_gap\": 2.1,\n      \"random_vs_r48_gap\": -2.2,\n      \"p_value_mb_vs_random\": 0.003,\n      \"p_value_mb_vs_r48\": 0.008,\n      \"p_value_mb_vs_r16\": \"<0.001\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Ablation ladder — LIBERO-Plus overall success rate (5 seeds)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Overall (%) | Std | Δ vs Frozen | Δ vs r48 |\\n|--------|--------|------------|-----|-------------|----------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 0 | 53.6 | 2.1 | — | −12.8 |\\n| Universal r16 | 3.7M | 62.1 | 1.8 | +8.5 | −4.3 |\\n| Random routing | 12M | 64.2 | 2.5 | +10.6 | −2.2 |\\n| Universal r48 | 12M | 66.4 | 1.6 | +12.8 | — |\\n| **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **72.0** | **1.9** | **+18.4** | **+5.6** |\\n| Oracle routing | 12M | 77.0 | 1.4 | +23.4 | +10.6 |\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Per-perturbation-category breakdown\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 |\\n| Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 |\\n| Random routing | 67.8 | 62.4 | 57.0 |\\n| Universal r48 | 70.1 | 64.3 | 58.6 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 |\\n| Oracle | 81.8 | 74.6 | 66.4 |\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Statistical significance (pairwise p-values, two-sided t-test, 5 seeds)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Comparison | Δ (pp) | p-value | Significant (α=0.01) |\\n|-----------|--------|---------|---------------------|\\n| MB vs Frozen | +18.4 | <0.001 | ✓ |\\n| MB vs r16 | +9.9 | <0.001 | ✓ |\\n| MB vs Random | +7.8 | 0.003 | ✓ |\\n| MB vs r48 | +5.6 | 0.008 | ✓ |\\n| Oracle vs MB | +5.0 | 0.011 | ✗ (marginal) |\\n| Random vs r16 | +2.1 | 0.14 | ✗ |\\n| Random vs r48 | −2.2 | 0.18 | ✗ |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ablation ladder is the paper's core figure. Critical narrative points: (1) Random < r48 is the surprise result — it proves that routing QUALITY matters, not just expert diversity. Without this, a reviewer could argue Meta-Brain's gains are just ensemble effects. (2) Meta-Brain vs r48 (+5.6pp, p=0.008) is statistically significant but requires careful framing: \\\"modest but reliable routing premium.\\\" (3) The per-type breakdown shows Physical compresses the ladder — all methods are closer together on Physical than on Visual/Spatial. This is the honest story: routing helps most where representation-level corrections differ qualitatively by type. (4) Oracle−MB significance is marginal (p=0.011) — the paper can discuss the oracle gap as suggestive rather than definitive.\\n\\n\\nThe ablation ladder forms a clean monotonic ordering: Frozen (53.6) < r16 (62.1) < Random (64.2) < r48 (66.4) < Meta-Brain (72.0) < Oracle (77.0). Each step isolates a specific factor:\\nFrozen→r16 (+8.5pp): LoRA adaptation on perturbed data helps, even with limited capacity and no specialization. This is the \\\"any correction is better than none\\\" baseline gain.\\nr16→Random (+2.1pp): Random routing gives 1/3 chance of the right expert, plus the ensemble effect of having 3× the parameters (12M vs 3.7M). The modest +2.1pp shows the ensemble effect alone is minor — randomly applying the wrong expert often hurts more than the extra capacity helps.\\nRandom→r48 (−2.2pp): Random routing actually underperforms the parameter-matched universal r48. This is important: 12M parameters concentrated in one coherent adapter (r48) outperform 12M parameters split across 3 randomly-assigned experts. Random routing's 2/3 mismatch penalty exceeds its 1/3 specialization benefit. This directly supports the claim that INFORMED routing is necessary — mere expert diversity without correct assignment is worse than a single universal adapter.\\nr48→Meta-Brain (+5.6pp): The routing premium. Informed routing turns the 3-expert architecture from a liability (random: −2.2pp vs r48) into a significant advantage (+5.6pp vs r48). This is the core evidence for Claim 2.\\nMeta-Brain→Oracle (+5.0pp): Room for routing improvement. The PAR router captures most of the value but soft blending leaves 5pp on the table.\\nStatistical significance: Meta-Brain vs random (p=0.003) and vs r48 (p=0.008) are significant at α=0.01 across 5 seeds. Random routing has the highest variance (std=2.5) due to stochastic expert assignment; oracle has the lowest (std=1.4) due to deterministic correct routing.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Reproduce FTM on pi0 backbone for visual perturbation types. Compare Meta-Brain's visual performance vs FTM. Establish that Meta-Brain matches visual-only methods on visual while additionally covering spatial/physical.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ftm_visual_overall\": 73.8,\n      \"ftm_camera\": 76.4,\n      \"ftm_light\": 78.2,\n      \"ftm_background\": 67.8,\n      \"ftm_noise\": 72.6,\n      \"ftm_spatial\": 52.4,\n      \"ftm_physical\": 47.8,\n      \"ftm_overall\": 60.8,\n      \"ftm_params_M\": 0.004,\n      \"meta_brain_visual_overall\": 75.5,\n      \"meta_brain_vs_ftm_visual_delta\": 1.7,\n      \"meta_brain_vs_ftm_spatial_delta\": 16.8,\n      \"meta_brain_vs_ftm_physical_delta\": 13.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"FTM vs Meta-Brain comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall |\\n|--------|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | 53.6 |\\n| FTM | 4K | 73.8 | 52.4 | 47.8 | 60.8 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 12M | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 |\\n| Δ (MB − FTM) | — | +1.7 | +16.8 | +13.2 | +11.2 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"FTM comparison reinforces the multi-type robustness narrative. Do NOT claim Meta-Brain beats FTM on visual — the 1.7pp gap is not significant and FTM achieves this with 3000× fewer parameters. Instead frame: \\\"Meta-Brain matches purpose-built visual methods while providing broad perturbation coverage that single-modality approaches cannot. The parameter cost (12M vs 4K) is the price of multi-type generality.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFTM is purpose-built for visual robustness with extreme parameter efficiency (~4K params), so it achieves strong 73.8% on visual perturbations — only 1.7pp below Meta-Brain's 75.5%. This is expected: FTM's fine-tuned visual transforms directly target the same representation corruption that LoRA-V corrects, and 4K purpose-designed parameters can be more efficient than 3.7M generic LoRA parameters for a narrow task. However, FTM on spatial (52.4%) and physical (47.8%) is at or below frozen pi0 level — its visual-only training provides zero spatial/physical correction and occasionally interferes (physical 47.8% vs frozen 47.2% suggests slight degradation from applying visual transforms to non-visual inputs). FTM's background swap result (67.8%) is notably lower than its camera/light/noise results because background swaps introduce novel textures that FTM's limited capacity cannot fully absorb. Meta-Brain's value proposition is clear: comparable visual performance (+1.7pp) plus +16.8pp on spatial and +13.2pp on physical that FTM cannot touch.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Parameter efficiency analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Generate scatter plot comparing Meta-Brain, Universal variants, FTM, VLA-GSE.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"meta_brain_gain_pp\": 18.4,\n      \"meta_brain_params_M\": 12,\n      \"meta_brain_gain_per_Mparam\": 1.53,\n      \"universal_r16_gain_pp\": 8.5,\n      \"universal_r16_params_M\": 3.7,\n      \"universal_r16_gain_per_Mparam\": 2.3,\n      \"universal_r48_gain_pp\": 12.8,\n      \"universal_r48_params_M\": 12,\n      \"universal_r48_gain_per_Mparam\": 1.07,\n      \"ftm_gain_pp_visual_only\": 16.0,\n      \"ftm_params_M\": 0.004,\n      \"ftm_gain_per_Mparam\": 4000.0,\n      \"ftm_gain_pp_overall\": 7.2,\n      \"ftm_gain_per_Mparam_overall\": 1800.0,\n      \"vla_gse_gain_pp\": 27.6,\n      \"vla_gse_params_M\": 114,\n      \"vla_gse_gain_per_Mparam\": 0.24,\n      \"vla_gse_note\": \"different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), indirect comparison only\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Parameter efficiency comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Params | Gain over frozen | Gain/Mparam | Coverage |\\n|--------|--------|-----------------|-------------|----------|\\n| FTM | 4K | +16.0pp (visual) | 4000.0 | Visual only |\\n| Universal r16 | 3.7M | +8.5pp | 2.30 | All (uniform) |\\n| **Meta-Brain** | **12M** | **+18.4pp** | **1.53** | **All (specialized)** |\\n| Universal r48 | 12M | +12.8pp | 1.07 | All (uniform) |\\n| VLA-GSE* | 114M | +27.6pp* | 0.24* | All (capacity) |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The scatter plot is the Claim 3 figure. Meta-Brain's position above the universal LoRA scaling line visually demonstrates the routing premium. However, FTM's dominance on visual-only efficiency needs honest treatment — acknowledge that for deployments facing only visual perturbations, FTM is more efficient. Meta-Brain's value is multi-type coverage with routing specialization. VLA-GSE comparison must carry the \\\"different backbone\\\" caveat prominently — apples-to-oranges with asterisk, not a direct parameter efficiency claim.\\n\\n\\nParameter efficiency depends on what you measure. FTM dominates gain-per-param on visual-only (4000 gain/Mparam) because it was designed for exactly this — but its overall gain-per-param (1800) still leads because overall averages include the visual types where FTM excels. Universal r16 has better gain-per-param (2.30) than Meta-Brain (1.53) because smaller models have an efficiency advantage on the low-hanging-fruit gains. The critical comparison is r48 (1.07) vs Meta-Brain (1.53): at the same parameter budget, routing gives 43% better efficiency than uniform adaptation. VLA-GSE at 0.24 gain/Mparam (on a different backbone, hence indirect) shows that Meta-Brain is ~6.4× more parameter-efficient in gain-per-param terms. The scatter plot will show Meta-Brain above the \\\"universal LoRA scaling line\\\" (connecting r16 and r48 points), visually demonstrating that routing adds value beyond what parameter scaling alone provides.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Correction Memory ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare Meta-Brain ± Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing weights based on FAISS-retrieved exemplars. Quantify marginal contribution.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"meta_brain_no_cm_overall\": 72.0,\n      \"meta_brain_with_cm_overall\": 73.2,\n      \"cm_delta_overall\": 1.2,\n      \"cm_delta_visual\": 1.4,\n      \"cm_delta_spatial\": 1.2,\n      \"cm_delta_physical\": 0.8,\n      \"cm_p_value\": 0.12,\n      \"cm_retrieval_latency_ms\": 2.3,\n      \"cm_alpha_learned\": 0.15\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Correction Memory ablation\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall | p-value |\\n|--------|--------|---------|----------|---------|---------|\\n| Meta-Brain | 75.5 | 69.2 | 61.0 | 72.0 | — |\\n| Meta-Brain + CM | 76.9 | 70.4 | 61.8 | 73.2 | 0.12 |\\n| Δ CM | +1.4 | +1.2 | +0.8 | +1.2 | n.s. |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"CM is confirmed as ablation footnote material. Present as: \\\"An optional Correction Memory module provides +1.2pp (n.s.) by modulating routing weights with retrieved exemplars. We include this result for completeness but recommend the CM-free architecture for deployment simplicity.\\\" Do NOT elevate CM in the architecture diagram or contribution list.\\n\\n\\nCM provides a marginal +1.2pp improvement that is not statistically significant (p=0.12 across 5 seeds). The learned alpha=0.15 means CM modulates routing weights by only 15% at most — the base PAR router already does most of the work. The per-type pattern follows the same gradient as all other results: visual benefits slightly more (+1.4pp) because visual perturbation exemplars are most distinctive in representation space and FAISS retrieval is most accurate. Physical benefits least (+0.8pp) because physical perturbation representations are less separable, so retrieved exemplars are noisier. The 2.3ms retrieval latency is negligible but the architectural complexity (FAISS index, exemplar storage, alpha parameter) is not justified by 1.2pp. Dream reviewers unanimously flagged CM as padding — this ablation confirms that assessment.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Router architecture comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare PAR router variants: 2-layer MLP (default), linear classifier, attention- based router. All on PaliGemma layer 6, first-token pooling.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"linear_routing_f1\": 0.988,\n      \"linear_downstream_success\": 70.6,\n      \"linear_params_M\": 0.1,\n      \"mlp_routing_f1\": 0.996,\n      \"mlp_downstream_success\": 72.0,\n      \"mlp_params_M\": 0.5,\n      \"attention_routing_f1\": 0.997,\n      \"attention_downstream_success\": 72.4,\n      \"attention_params_M\": 0.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Router architecture comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| Router | Params | Routing F1 | Downstream (%) | Δ vs MLP |\\n|--------|--------|-----------|----------------|----------|\\n| Linear | 0.1M | 0.988 | 70.6 | −1.4 |\\n| 2-layer MLP | 0.5M | 0.996 | 72.0 | — |\\n| Attention | 0.8M | 0.997 | 72.4 | +0.4 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Router architecture is not a bottleneck. Present as a single sentence in the ablation section: \\\"We compared linear, MLP, and attention-based routers; the 2-layer MLP provides the best complexity-performance tradeoff (Table N).\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe routing classification task is nearly saturated (Phase 0 F1=0.993), so router architecture differences are small. Linear classifier's slight accuracy gap (F1 0.988 vs 0.996) translates to ~1.4pp downstream loss: the 0.8% additional routing errors occur on ambiguous boundary cases (e.g., background swaps that also affect spatial layout), and these errors compound over multi-step rollouts (a single misroute early in an episode can cascade). Attention-based router marginally improves (F1 0.997, +0.4pp downstream) by better handling these boundary cases through cross-dimensional attention on the 2048-dim PaliGemma features, but the gain doesn't justify 60% more parameters. The 2-layer MLP is Pareto-optimal: near-perfect routing at minimal overhead.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"6-class vs 3-class routing\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot, 6 LoRA experts) vs 3-class coarse routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"class3_overall\": 72.0,\n      \"class3_params_M\": 12,\n      \"class6_overall\": 72.6,\n      \"class6_params_M\": 24,\n      \"marginal_gain_per_expert\": 0.2,\n      \"class6_camera\": 79.3,\n      \"class6_light\": 80.0,\n      \"class6_background\": 72.8,\n      \"class6_noise\": 75.0,\n      \"class6_layout\": 69.6,\n      \"class6_robot\": 61.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"6-class vs 3-class routing\",\n        \"content\": \"| Routing | Experts | Params | Overall | Δ vs 3-class |\\n|---------|---------|--------|---------|-------------|\\n| 3-class | 3 | 12M | 72.0 | — |\\n| 6-class | 6 | 24M | 72.6 | +0.6 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Directly addresses reviewer concern C5. 3-class is sufficient — 6-class doubles parameters for 0.6pp. Background swap is the only subtype that meaningfully benefits from its own expert, but not enough to justify the complexity. If a reviewer pushes, point to Phase 0 intra-Visual clustering as the mechanistic justification for grouping.\\n\\n\\n6-class routing with 6 experts provides only +0.6pp over 3-class at 2× the parameters. This is expected from Phase 0: intra-Visual subtypes cluster tightly in activation space (6-class accuracy 99.82% shows they're separable, but 3-class accuracy 99.46% shows the grouping is already natural). The marginal gain per additional expert (0.2pp) is negligible. Within visual subtypes, background swap benefits most from its own expert (+1.8pp: 72.8 vs 71.0) because background textures are the most visually distinct subtype, but camera/light/noise show <1pp gains over being grouped. Spatial and physical are already singleton categories so gain is only from having dedicated visual subtype experts freeing up minor capacity. The 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing (early/mid/late layers) and is the right granularity.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LoRA rank sweep (r=16/32/64) — capacity-routing interaction\",\n    \"description\": \"Test whether routing benefit grows with expert capacity. At each rank R, compare 3 type-specific rank-R experts with PAR routing vs parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank 3R). Measures capacity-routing interaction.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r16_routed_overall\": 72.0,\n      \"r16_universal_matched_overall\": 66.4,\n      \"r16_routing_benefit\": 5.6,\n      \"r32_routed_overall\": 75.4,\n      \"r32_universal_matched_overall\": 68.6,\n      \"r32_routing_benefit\": 6.8,\n      \"r64_routed_overall\": 78.2,\n      \"r64_universal_matched_overall\": 70.4,\n      \"r64_routing_benefit\": 7.8,\n      \"capacity_threshold\": \"r≥32\",\n      \"r32_total_params_M\": 24,\n      \"r64_total_params_M\": 48\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Rank sweep: routing benefit vs expert capacity\",\n        \"content\": \"| Expert Rank | 3×Expert (Routed) | Universal (Matched) | Routing Δ | Total Params |\\n|-------------|-------------------|--------------------|-----------|----|\\n| r=16 | 72.0% | 66.4% | +5.6pp | 12M |\\n| r=32 | 75.4% | 68.6% | +6.8pp | 24M |\\n| r=64 | 78.2% | 70.4% | +7.8pp | 48M |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The rank sweep adds a concrete finding to the paper: \\\"routing benefit scales with expert capacity, with an inflection at r≥32.\\\" At our operating point (r16), routing benefit is 5.6pp — present as the minimum viable routing advantage. The implication: practitioners with larger compute budgets should increase expert rank to maximize the routing premium. The r64 routed result (78.2%) exceeding the r16 oracle (77.0%) is a nice finding — higher-capacity experts + learned routing can surpass perfect-routing of lower-capacity experts. Frame as: \\\"capacity and routing are complementary dimensions of adaptation.\\\"\\n\\n\\nRouting benefit is capacity-dependent, growing from 5.6pp (r16) to 6.8pp (r32) to 7.8pp (r64). The mechanism: at low rank (r16), each expert has limited capacity to specialize — the rank-16 LoRA subspace is constrained enough that specialized and universal corrections partially overlap (both learn \\\"general robustness\\\" corrections that consume most of the limited rank budget, leaving little room for type-specific corrections). At rank 32, experts have enough capacity to learn the general correction AND type-specific refinements — this is where specialization becomes meaningfully different from universality, producing the inflection from 5.6→6.8pp. At rank 64, further capacity allows even finer specialization but with diminishing marginal routing benefit (6.8→7.8pp, only +1.0pp more). The universal LoRA scaling line (66.4→68.6→70.4) shows steady but decelerating gains from rank alone, while the routed line (72.0→75.4→78.2) scales faster because additional rank is leveraged differently by each expert. At r64, the routed system (78.2%) approaches the rank-16 oracle level (77.0%), suggesting that higher-capacity experts can partially compensate for imperfect routing. The capacity threshold of r≥32 is a concrete, actionable finding: \\\"routing requires a minimum expert capacity to be beneficial beyond parameter scaling.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Clean LIBERO evaluation — nominal performance preservation\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate Meta-Brain on standard (unperturbed) LIBERO benchmark. Verify that routing does not significantly degrade nominal performance.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"frozen_pi0_clean_libero\": 92.4,\n      \"meta_brain_clean_libero\": 90.4,\n      \"clean_delta_pp\": -2.0,\n      \"universal_r16_clean_libero\": 91.6,\n      \"universal_r48_clean_libero\": 91.2,\n      \"oracle_clean_libero\": 91.0,\n      \"par_entropy_on_clean\": 0.88,\n      \"par_max_weight_on_clean\": 0.48,\n      \"par_routing_distribution_clean\": \"roughly uniform (0.35, 0.31, 0.34)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Clean LIBERO performance\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method | Clean LIBERO (%) | Δ vs Frozen |\\n|--------|-----------------|-------------|\\n| Frozen pi0 | 92.4 | — |\\n| Universal r16 | 91.6 | −0.8 |\\n| Universal r48 | 91.2 | −1.2 |\\n| Oracle routing | 91.0 | −1.4 |\\n| Meta-Brain | 90.4 | −2.0 |\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"PAR router behavior on clean inputs\",\n        \"content\": \"| Metric | Value |\\n|--------|-------|\\n| Avg entropy | 0.88 |\\n| Max expert weight | 0.48 |\\n| w(LoRA-V) | 0.35 |\\n| w(LoRA-S) | 0.31 |\\n| w(LoRA-P) | 0.34 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The -2.0pp clean degradation is notable but manageable. The entropy analysis provides a clean solution: confidence-gated routing. Add to the paper as a practical recommendation: \\\"For deployment with mixed clean/perturbed inputs, we recommend entropy-gated routing — suppress corrections when PAR entropy exceeds 0.8 (indicating no clear perturbation type). This recovers frozen performance on clean inputs while preserving routing benefits on perturbed inputs.\\\" Include the entropy histogram as evidence that the gating threshold is well-defined (bimodal distribution with clear separation).\\n\\n\\nMeta-Brain shows -2.0pp degradation on clean LIBERO (90.4% vs frozen 92.4%). The mechanism: on clean inputs, no perturbation correction is needed, but the PAR router still activates with non-trivial confidence. PAR entropy on clean inputs is high (0.88 — near maximum entropy of ln(3)=1.10) and the routing distribution is roughly uniform (0.35/0.31/0.34), meaning the router is uncertain about which type to route to (correct behavior — there IS no perturbation type). However, the near-uniform routing still applies a weighted average of all three expert corrections (each contributing ~33%), introducing small representation shifts that are not correcting any actual perturbation. These spurious corrections occasionally push the policy off its optimal trajectory, causing -2.0pp degradation. Universal LoRA variants show smaller degradation (-0.8pp for r16, -1.2pp for r48) because their single correction direction is less disruptive when unnecessary. Oracle routing on clean inputs (assigning a \\\"clean\\\" label that applies no correction) would be 92.4% by definition, but since oracle routing in practice assigns perturbation types (not a \\\"clean\\\" class), oracle on clean inputs must force-assign a type, yielding 91.0% (-1.4pp). This motivates confidence-gated routing: if PAR entropy exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.8), suppress expert corrections and fall back to frozen representations.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-backbone validation — OpenVLA\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate Phase 0 (perturbation classification) + core Phase 1 pipeline on OpenVLA to test generalizability of perturbation-diagnostic routing across architectures.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"openvla_phase0_3class_f1\": 0.94,\n      \"openvla_phase0_best_layer\": \"layer_8\",\n      \"openvla_phase0_k1_f1\": 0.86,\n      \"openvla_frozen_overall\": 48.2,\n      \"openvla_universal_r16_overall\": 55.8,\n      \"openvla_meta_brain_overall\": 61.4,\n      \"openvla_routing_benefit\": 5.6,\n      \"openvla_gain_over_frozen\": 13.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cross-backbone comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| Metric | pi0 (PaliGemma) | OpenVLA (Llama) |\\n|--------|-----------------|-----------------|\\n| Phase 0 3-class F1 | 0.993 | 0.94 |\\n| Phase 0 K=1 F1 | 0.898 | 0.86 |\\n| Best classification layer | 6 | 8 |\\n| Frozen baseline | 53.6% | 48.2% |\\n| Universal r16 | 62.1% | 55.8% |\\n| Meta-Brain | 72.0% | 61.4% |\\n| Routing Δ (vs universal r16) | +9.9pp | +5.6pp |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-backbone results support generalizability with caveats. The routing principle works on OpenVLA but with lower absolute numbers and less clean separation. Present as: \\\"perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes across VLA architectures — the routing benefit (5-6pp over universal LoRA) is consistent despite different backbone feature characteristics.\\\" Honestly note that OpenVLA's weaker Phase 0 classification (F1=0.94) sets a lower ceiling for routing quality. This is a P2 experiment — if time is short, Phase 0 replication alone is sufficient for a generalizability claim.\\n\\n\\nPhase 0 partially replicates on OpenVLA: 3-class F1=0.94 is lower than pi0's 0.993 because OpenVLA's Llama backbone produces less cleanly separable perturbation representations than PaliGemma (Llama processes visual tokens through more language-oriented layers, partially mixing perturbation signals). Best layer shifts from layer 6 (pi0/PaliGemma) to layer 8 (OpenVLA/Llama), consistent with different architecture depths. K=1 F1 drops more sharply (0.86 vs 0.898) because single-step features are noisier in OpenVLA. Phase 1 routing benefit on OpenVLA (~5.6pp over universal r16) is comparable to pi0's 5.6pp over r48, suggesting the routing principle generalizes even if absolute performance differs (OpenVLA's frozen baseline is lower at 48.2%). The similar routing benefit across backbones is the key generalizability result — perturbation- diagnostic routing is not pi0-specific.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LoRA layer placement ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers (default). Test per-expert-type layer placement hypothesis.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"lora_v_early_visual\": 76.8,\n      \"lora_v_mid_visual\": 73.2,\n      \"lora_v_late_visual\": 70.4,\n      \"lora_v_all_visual\": 75.5,\n      \"lora_s_early_spatial\": 64.6,\n      \"lora_s_mid_spatial\": 70.8,\n      \"lora_s_late_spatial\": 66.2,\n      \"lora_s_all_spatial\": 69.2,\n      \"lora_p_early_physical\": 57.4,\n      \"lora_p_mid_physical\": 59.2,\n      \"lora_p_late_physical\": 60.8,\n      \"lora_p_all_physical\": 61.0,\n      \"optimal_v\": \"early (2-6)\",\n      \"optimal_s\": \"mid (6-12)\",\n      \"optimal_p\": \"late (12-16) ≈ all layers\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Layer placement ablation (per-type success rate with matched expert)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Expert | Early (2-6) | Mid (6-12) | Late (12-16) | All (default) | Best |\\n|--------|-------------|------------|-------------|---------------|------|\\n| LoRA-V on Visual | **76.8** | 73.2 | 70.4 | 75.5 | Early |\\n| LoRA-S on Spatial | 64.6 | **70.8** | 66.2 | 69.2 | Mid |\\n| LoRA-P on Physical | 57.4 | 59.2 | 60.8 | **61.0** | All ≈ Late |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Layer placement provides a practical insight for the paper's \\\"layer-perturbation mapping\\\" narrative. V→early and S→mid are clear wins that align with VLA processing stages. P→late/all is ambiguous, reinforcing that physical perturbation correction at the representation level is fundamentally limited. Present as: \\\"perturbation-type-to-layer mapping reveals that visual perturbations are best corrected at early layers (pre-spatial-reasoning) and spatial perturbations at mid layers (spatial-reasoning stage), suggesting type-aware layer assignment as a parameter efficiency optimization.\\\" Note: all-layer (default) is competitive for all types, so the default config is a reasonable choice if practitioners don't want to tune layer assignment.\\n\\nLayer placement shows a partial match to the V→early, S→mid, P→late hypothesis, but with important nuances. LoRA-V is best at early layers (76.8%) because visual perturbations corrupt early representations most — intervening at layers 2-6 corrects corrupted visual features before they propagate to higher layers. LoRA-V at all layers (75.5%) is slightly worse because later LoRA modules introduce unnecessary corrections at layers where visual features are already clean. LoRA-S is best at mid layers (70.8%) because spatial perturbations (layout changes) affect mid-level spatial reasoning where object relationships are encoded — early layers still process raw visual features correctly, and late layers have already committed to action plans. LoRA-P shows the weakest layer preference: late layers (60.8%) are marginally best but barely exceed all layers (61.0%), confirming that physical perturbation correction is weak throughout the representation hierarchy. The lack of strong layer preference for LoRA-P reinforces the median-tier narrative that physical perturbations are poorly addressed at the representation level. The layer placement analysis provides a concrete practical insight: type-specific layer assignment (V→early, S→mid) could improve parameter efficiency by 40% (5 layers × 3 experts = 15 LoRA modules vs 18 layers × 3 experts = 54 modules in the default config).\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":404,"startLine":1,"totalLines":404}}}
